\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan Bonds Up; 10-Yr Yld Support At 1.21%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 07:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds up, tracking rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight, while investors turn cautious as they try to figure out how \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will affect TWD, and overall domestic economy, says trader from local brokerage. Adds, bonds unlikely to rise sharply with no strong lead from local stock market to help affirm clear direction. Still, "the economic outlook is not great so the new 10-year bond yield should remain low." Tips 1.20%-1.25% range at 10-year bond auction tomorrow. 10-year yield last at 1.2260% vs 1.2401% yesterday, tipped in 1.21%-1.23% Thursday. 5-year yield last at 0.8850% vs 0.8860%. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD steady after drifting off all day from overnight high of 0.7396; "It seems to be stabilizing at the moment, and I think it is probably still a buy on dips, but you may get support around 0.7270 and that may be a bit of an opportunity," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  NZ Chief Manager Daniel Brdanovic. Pair last at 0.7321. Says NZD, AUS, EUR likely to find further support after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY earlier and "it seems to be a risk on environment at the moment." Tips initial resistance at overnight high. Says market expecting RBNZ to remain on hold at tomorrow's rate review and while may be dovish on economy given weak data of late, earthquake, failure of South Canterbury Finance, may be more upbeat about what's happening offshore. "Net-net, it may be a fairly balanced outlook." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0459 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is encouraging as it shows increased attention to risks to economic cycle; says implication is that other complementary policies might also be seen. But adds, European and U.S. officials unlikely to be thrilled by Japan's action. Says there is a risk that if U.S. embarks on another quantitative easing that "we end up in a developed world beggar-thy-neighbour liquidity war. This could be very inflationary for developing world risk assets and commodity prices." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to hike rates three times this year including 25 basis points at mid-quarter review Thursday, says Daiwa Capital Markets in report. "We view inflationary expectations as well-entrenched now, and needing to be wrestled lower," says house; adds, real rates in economy deeply negative. House expects further 25 basis points hike in repo rate in next two scheduled policy reviews. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, supported by gains in mining, utility stocks; benchmark PSEi ends +0.1% at 3973.48 in heavy volume. Market breadth positive with gainers beating losers 74 to 64. Among leading gainers, telecom firm PLDT (TEL.PH) +1.4% at PHP2,498, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) +2.1% at PHP5.78, power firm First Gen (FGEN.PH) +4.3% at PHP13.62. Profit-taking hits Banco de Oro (BDO.PH), down 0.7% to PHP60.50, Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), down 1.1% to PHP73. Joseph Roxas, president of Eagle Equities, says foreign funds continue to flow into market with mining plays receiving rotational interest. Tips 3900-4000 range in near term. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF bought USD/JPY above 85.00, say 3 dealers at major financial firms; pair has gone as high as 85.14, and last at 85.10. One says sharp move above 85.00 would be difficult, though, due to "massive...I mean massive" offers from Japanese exporters; "naturally when we have this much selling offers the dollar can't rise (much) above 85.00." Notes further resistance at 86.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] As India's quest for coal heats up, it may need to lock horns more tightly with bigger neighbor China to secure enough supply of fuel to feed its fast-expanding economy. India, China consumed over 60% of global coal output last year, vast additions of new thermal-power capacity, steel-making plants likely to continue driving demand over at least next 2 decades. Much depends on both countries' strategies to secure overseas coal supplies or make most of their own deposits. However, India's strategy of relying ever more heavily on traditional suppliers may be unsustainable. Indonesia's government wants to conserve more of its coal output for domestic use, and labor, transport issues persist in South Africa. "Much like in Indonesia, where the government has issued a domestic market obligation to protect the country's domestic interests thereby placing restrictions on coal exports, countries like South Africa and Russia may suffer from the same predicament," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . See: =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: India, China Vie For Coal Market Supremacy (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1470.72 midday on profit-taking after recent highs, ahead of national holiday Thursday, dealer says. Tuesday, KLCI ended at fresh 32-month high of 1,474.44. "There is some profit-taking in blue-chips after the rally over the last few weeks," says dealer; tips for 1,465-1,474 range for rest of session. Among decliners Jerneh (6394.KU) down 5.5% at MYR3.10 on concerns over company's future following sale of core insurance business; AMMB (1015.KU) down 1.3% at MYR5.90, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.5% at MYR9.94. Among gainers Gamuda (5398.KU) +0.8% at MYR3.73 after news of being picked to be KLCI component stock from Sept. 20, TA Ann Holdings (5012.KU) +1.5% at MYR4.82. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY still down midday but off 6.7330 hit earlier, which was lowest intraday level under current system, as USD rebounds vs majors following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up USD/JPY; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7374 vs 6.7463 last close and tipped in 6.7300-6.7400 range rest of day. "The market is still dominated by dollar demand from customers today despite the lower fixing," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank; "the current level is good for dollar buyers, (while) businesses with yuan needs (like exporters) are holding off making transactions in anticipation the dollar may rebound against the yuan soon." Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6570/6.6620 vs intraday low around 6.6319, 6.6550/6.6600 yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 up 0.8% at 4662.3, extends gains to fresh 4-month high of 4665.3, as AUD/JPY has risen to 79.69 from 77.94 after MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY earlier. Traders expect MOF move to prompt funds to flow to risk assets, including equities. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen is pushing the Australian share market higher," says RBS head of sales Justin Gallagher. Adds that S&P/ASX 200 looks poised for further gains to 4800 in coming days. "September is shaping up as a good month and I think there's every chance we will find ourselves tickling that 4800 level." Next resistance 4700 (61.8% retracement of April/May decline). Support tentatively at 4650. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's authorities unlikely to try and push USD/JPY to 90.00 level, even though they may keep up their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in coming days, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Notes, international criticism may be biggest barrier for extending the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move towards 90.00; cites pace and strength of U.S. economic recovery (with negative U.S. news being JPY-positive), as well as how large hedging needs of Japanese exporters are, as other key considerations for Japan. "However, if USD/JPY falls back to 83.00, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prevent further JPY appreciation. As such, Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be defensive, rather than aggressive." Also reckons fact that there was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all "suggests that Japanese officials succeeded in persuading U.S. authorities that Japan had to act." USD/JPY last at 85.07, well above intraday low at 82.85. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] Activity in Singapore stock market robust as players dabble in small caps, lower liners. Volume above-average at 1.66 billion shares worth S$1.0 billion vs whole of yesterday's 2.19 billion worth S$1.82 billion. FTSE ST Small Cap Index +1.1%, FTSE ST Catalist Index +1.6%. Blue chips also higher but gains smaller, with STI +0.5% at 3063.05. If year-to-date high of 3068 gives way, next resistance for STI expected at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "The Singapore market should continue its 'band trading' as investors' sentiment fluctuate between fear of a double dip and relief of a 'soft landing' scenario," says DBS Vickers. Most active stocks in market include \uc2 China Hongxing Sports\uc2  (BR9.SG), +7.5% at S$0.215, Informatics (I03.SG), +9.1% at S$0.18, Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), +4.2% at S$1.74, Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +2.1% at S$1.95, China Sports (FQ8.SG), +4.0% at S$0.13. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0119ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
