offers the dollar can't rise (much) above 85.00." Notes further resistance at 86.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] As India's quest for coal heats up, it may need to lock horns more tightly with bigger neighbor China to secure enough supply of fuel to feed its fast-expanding economy. India, China consumed over 60% of global coal output last year, vast additions of new thermal-power capacity, steel-making plants likely to continue driving demand over at least next 2 decades. Much depends on both countries' strategies to secure overseas coal supplies or make most of their own deposits. However, India's strategy of relying ever more heavily on traditional suppliers may be unsustainable. Indonesia's government wants to conserve more of its coal output for domestic use, and labor, transport issues persist in South Africa. "Much like in Indonesia, where the government has issued a domestic market obligation to protect the country's domestic interests thereby placing restrictions on coal exports, countries like South Africa and Russia may suffer from the same predicament," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . See: =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: India, China Vie For Coal Market Supremacy (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1470.72 midday on profit-taking after recent highs, ahead of national holiday Thursday, dealer says. Tuesday, KLCI ended at fresh 32-month high of 1,474.44. "There is some profit-taking in blue-chips after the rally over the last few weeks," says dealer; tips for 1,465-1,474 range for rest of session. Among decliners Jerneh (6394.KU) down 5.5% at MYR3.10 on concerns over company's future following sale of core insurance business; AMMB (1015.KU) down 1.3% at MYR5.90, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.5% at MYR9.94. Among gainers Gamuda (5398.KU) +0.8% at MYR3.73 after news of being picked to be KLCI component stock from Sept. 20, TA Ann Holdings (5012.KU) +1.5% at MYR4.82. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY still down midday but off 6.7330 hit earlier, which was lowest intraday level under current system, as USD rebounds vs majors following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up USD/JPY; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7374 vs 6.7463 last close and tipped in 6.7300-6.7400 range rest of day. "The market is still dominated by dollar demand from customers today despite the lower fixing," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank; "the current level is good for dollar buyers, (while) businesses with yuan needs (like exporters) are holding off making transactions in anticipation the dollar may rebound against the yuan soon." Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6570/6.6620 vs intraday low around 6.6319, 6.6550/6.6600 yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 up 0.8% at 4662.3, extends gains to fresh 4-month high of 4665.3, as AUD/JPY has risen to 79.69 from 77.94 after MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY earlier. Traders expect MOF move to prompt funds to flow to risk assets, including equities. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen is pushing the Australian share market higher," says RBS head of sales Justin Gallagher. Adds that S&P/ASX 200 looks poised for further gains to 4800 in coming days. "September is shaping up as a good month and I think there's every chance we will find ourselves tickling that 4800 level." Next resistance 4700 (61.8% retracement of April/May decline). Support tentatively at 4650. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's authorities unlikely to try and push USD/JPY to 90.00 level, even though they may keep up their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in coming days, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Notes, international criticism may be biggest barrier for extending the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move towards 90.00; cites pace and strength of U.S. economic recovery (with negative U.S. news being JPY-positive), as well as how large hedging needs of Japanese exporters are, as other key considerations for Japan. "However, if USD/JPY falls back to 83.00, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prevent further JPY appreciation. As such, Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be defensive, rather than aggressive." Also reckons fact that there was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all "suggests that Japanese officials succeeded in persuading U.S. authorities that Japan had to act." USD/JPY last at 85.07, well above intraday low at 82.85. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] Activity in Singapore stock market robust as players dabble in small caps, lower liners. Volume above-average at 1.66 billion shares worth S$1.0 billion vs whole of yesterday's 2.19 billion worth S$1.82 billion. FTSE ST Small Cap Index +1.1%, FTSE ST Catalist Index +1.6%. Blue chips also higher but gains smaller, with STI +0.5% at 3063.05. If year-to-date high of 3068 gives way, next resistance for STI expected at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "The Singapore market should continue its 'band trading' as investors' sentiment fluctuate between fear of a double dip and relief of a 'soft landing' scenario," says DBS Vickers. Most active stocks in market include \uc2 China Hongxing Sports\uc2  (BR9.SG), +7.5% at S$0.215, Informatics (I03.SG), +9.1% at S$0.18, Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), +4.2% at S$1.74, Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +2.1% at S$1.95, China Sports (FQ8.SG), +4.0% at S$0.13. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 0.1% at 448.72 on concerns about equity dilution after state media reports local banks plan to issue VND80 trillion worth of new shares between now and year-end. "Many players are hesitant to buy and they are hoping that the government will soon come up with specific measures to support stock prices," says trader. Notes key index lost 12% so far this quarter. Volume moderate with 38.7 million shares valued at VND1.02 trillion exchanging hands. Among most active, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends +4% at VND34,000, SSI (OGC.VH) ends down 1.1% at VND27,000, REE (REE.VH) down 1.2% at VND16,500. Traders tip key index support at 445. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI near flat at 21,706.14 midday, consolidating after risen 9 of past 10 sessions, also as investors' sentiment slightly dented by slew of fund-raising. Volume stays solid at HK$37.71 billion, but boosted from combined HK$3.67 billion from China Resources Gas (1193.HK), United Lab (3933.HK) post share placement. Still, more short-term upside likely after consolidation; SHK Financial says HSI broke above medium-term downtrend Tuesday, indicating strong technical trend; tips next HSI target early August high of 21,800. Blue chips mostly rangebound amid consolidation; \uc2 China Resources Enterprise\uc2  (0291.HK) off 2.2% at HK$32.80, succumbing to profit-taking after setting 52-week high yesterday, is only index member to fall (or rise) by more than 2.0%. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures on roller-coaster ride in afternoon; contract hit intraday low of 141.58 at beginning of afternoon session but rebounds sharply to last around 141.87. While players initially sold off JGBs on surprise that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to push down JPY, demand for JGBs remains strong as fiscal concerns recede after PM Kan beats fiscal expansionist Ozawa at yesterday's DPJ presidential election, and with no more long-term JGB auctions planned this month, says Tokyo-based fund manager. Expects 10-year cash JGB yield to decline to around 1.0% in near term vs last down 7.0 bps at 1.060%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0056ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
