\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HSI Near Flat; More Upside After Consolidation\b0\par\par\uc2 1,607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 06:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI near flat at 21,706.14 midday, consolidating after risen 9 of past 10 sessions, also as investors' sentiment slightly dented by slew of fund-raising. Volume stays solid at HK$37.71 billion, but boosted from combined HK$3.67 billion from China Resources Gas (1193.HK), United Lab (3933.HK) post share placement. Still, more short-term upside likely after consolidation; SHK Financial says HSI broke above medium-term downtrend Tuesday, indicating strong technical trend; tips next HSI target early August high of 21,800. Blue chips mostly rangebound amid consolidation; \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Resources Enterprise\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0291.HK) off 2.2% at HK$32.80, succumbing to profit-taking after setting 52-week high yesterday, is only index member to fall (or rise) by more than 2.0%. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures on roller-coaster ride in afternoon; contract hit intraday low of 141.58 at beginning of afternoon session but rebounds sharply to last around 141.87. While players initially sold off JGBs on surprise that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to push down JPY, demand for JGBs remains strong as fiscal concerns recede after PM Kan beats fiscal expansionist Ozawa at yesterday's DPJ presidential election, and with no more long-term JGB auctions planned this month, says Tokyo-based fund manager. Expects 10-year cash JGB yield to decline to around 1.0% in near term vs last down 7.0 bps at 1.060%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0430 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options higher at 12.45%/13.15% vs 11.85%/12.55% New York Tuesday, as spot surges past 85.00 in Asia morning after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market for first time in over 6 years, prompting investors to buy hedges against sharp fluctuations in short-term. In particular, investors buy USD-call/JPY-put contracts for anywhere between 1-week to 2-month term in expectation that pair may rise further, says options dealer at major brokerage in Tokyo. One player buys 1-month USD-call/JPY-put at 85.00 with implied volatilities of 12.7% for about $30 million, the dealer says. Adds, markets paying close attention to any further moves by Japan authorities as trading shifts to London, New York from Tokyo.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 flat at 3197.45 as strength in Fisher & Paykel Appliance (FPA.NZ), countered by weakness in Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Adrian Vance; "How the market closes will depend very much on the moves of Fletcher Building and Telecom this afternoon." Fletcher Building down 0.6% at NZ$8.43 after strong rally in last ten days. Adds Steel & Tube (STU.NZ) down NZ$0.05, or 0.8% at NZ$2.55, after going ex-dividend of NZ$0.07 so technically higher on ongoing strength after Christchurch earthquake last week. \uc2 NZ Oil & Gas\uc2  down 3.8% at NZ$1.27, Port of Tauranga down 3.2% at NZ$6.65 after going ex-dividend today. Says \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  (FPA.NZ) +3.6% at NZ$0.58 after announcing it had developed new compressor technology that could save fridge users up to 30% in power; "The stock has recovered quite nicely from the lows but obviously it is a minor move in the scheme of what has happened to that company in the last couple of months." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0417 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 2.8% at 9555.21 after rising as high as 3.0% in afternoon session on continuing rise in USD/JPY (now 84.90) following Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Since the impression had been that (Prime Minister) Kan was late in taking measures to address the stronger yen, the timing of this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lead to a significant boost in investor sentiment," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  equity strategist Fumiyuki Takahashi. Puts Nikkei resistance for today at 9600, but adds index may rise to 10,000 by year-end. Says investors turning attention to whether government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be followed up by further easing measures by BOJ and speedy implementation of growth strategies, including reduction of corporate taxes. All Topix subindexes higher; Fanuc (6954.TO) up 4.0% at Y10,130, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 4.2% at Y3,020, \uc2 Nikon\uc2  (7731.TO) up 5.3% at Y1,483. Select financial shares remain down; \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) off 1.5% at Y66, Resona Bank (8308.TO) off 0.7% at Y850, \uc2 Nomura Holdings\uc2  (8604.TO) off 0.2% at Y457. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. Treasurys little moved, in Asia trade, by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; 10-year yield currently at 2.695% vs 2.663% at NY close overnight. However, U.S. government bonds theoretically likely to remain supported by Japan MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move as well as speculation that Fed may resume purchases of government bonds, says Tokyo-based fund manager. Tips 10-year U.S. Treasury yield to stay in 2.5%-2.8% range in near term.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.6% at 19484.62, likely to extend winning streak to 7 sessions today as institutions buy, gains in most regional markets support. 26 of the 30 index issues trade up with \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY)\uc2 , +2.0% at INR3,036.10, RIL (500325.BY), +1.3% at INR1,000.65 leading gains. "The opening is better-than expected. Uptick in buying interest by overseas funds in the last 2 sessions and initial July-Sept corporate advance tax numbers kicking-in, look strong and are aiding markets," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, software stocks draw decent buying interest for second straight day. Tips strong resistance at 19,530. ONGC (500312.BY) last +1.8% at INR1,421, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1.1% at INR603.20. Wipro (507685.BY) +1.8% at INR425, TCS (532540.BY) +1% at INR903.80. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad up, likely rangebound in session as dealers avoid long positions ahead of monetary policy review Thursday, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at 99.05 vs 99.01 at Tuesday's close. "Markets are likely to sustain current levels. A 25 basis points hike in rates is already factored in," says dealer. Tips yields on 2020 bond in 7.93%-7.97% range, last 7.94%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tad lower, tracking broad USD weakness overnight but news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep pair in narrow range, says dealer with private bank; pair at 46.41 vs 46.46 late Tuesday. Following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , players are cautious because they think USD could strengthen, says dealer. Reckons RBI unlikely to follow Japan MOF's steps as INR gains have been contained. Tips pair in 46.30-46.50 range. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds lower with selling due to news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY, which prompts suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Profit-taking after recent gains also driven by players skeptical of talk in market that Thai authorities may impose some measures to curb THB rise, says local dealer. Adds, yields may drift higher later when European markets open. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.72%/2.69% vs 2.70%/2.68% late Tuesday, November 2016 quoted at 2.84%/2.80% vs 2.83%/2.78%, June 2019 quoted at 2.94%/2.89% vs 2.92%/2.87%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses midday as banks pressured by continued concerns over prospect of rate hike from PBOC. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.6% at 2673.08, tipped to stay in tight 2650-2750 band rest of session. "This month may be the turning point for China's rising inflation, but investors still haven't ruled out the possibility of an interest rate hike," says Zhang Gang at Central China Securities. Says cautious mood may extend to mid-October, when September's macroeconomic data released, as few trading days left this month due to holidays. Bank of Communications (601328.SH) down 0.9% at CNY5.80, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) down 1.2% at CNY13.20. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.9% at 1196.96. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [Nikkei/Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  equity strategist Shun Maruyama says Japanese government's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market praiseworthy, but says some in market speculate long lasting effect may require bigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cooperation from other countries. That said, says even if yen's weakness is short-lived, government's decisive action sent message that it will act if/when necessary, and may take more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps from now on, which may discourage continued yen buying. Nikkei resistance seen at 9500 level. USD/JPY at 84.92, well up from sub-83 level seen earlier. Nikkei up 3.0% at 9576.13, up from midday close of 9470.31. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0040ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
