uesday. Following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , players are cautious because they think USD could strengthen, says dealer. Reckons RBI unlikely to follow Japan MOF's steps as INR gains have been contained. Tips pair in 46.30-46.50 range. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds lower with selling due to news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY, which prompts suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Profit-taking after recent gains also driven by players skeptical of talk in market that Thai authorities may impose some measures to curb THB rise, says local dealer. Adds, yields may drift higher later when European markets open. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.72%/2.69% vs 2.70%/2.68% late Tuesday, November 2016 quoted at 2.84%/2.80% vs 2.83%/2.78%, June 2019 quoted at 2.94%/2.89% vs 2.92%/2.87%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses midday as banks pressured by continued concerns over prospect of rate hike from PBOC. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.6% at 2673.08, tipped to stay in tight 2650-2750 band rest of session. "This month may be the turning point for China's rising inflation, but investors still haven't ruled out the possibility of an interest rate hike," says Zhang Gang at Central China Securities. Says cautious mood may extend to mid-October, when September's macroeconomic data released, as few trading days left this month due to holidays. Bank of Communications (601328.SH) down 0.9% at CNY5.80, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) down 1.2% at CNY13.20. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.9% at 1196.96. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [Nikkei/Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  equity strategist Shun Maruyama says Japanese government's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market praiseworthy, but says some in market speculate long lasting effect may require bigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cooperation from other countries. That said, says even if yen's weakness is short-lived, government's decisive action sent message that it will act if/when necessary, and may take more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps from now on, which may discourage continued yen buying. Nikkei resistance seen at 9500 level. USD/JPY at 84.92, well up from sub-83 level seen earlier. Nikkei up 3.0% at 9576.13, up from midday close of 9470.31. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rebounds strongly on active USD buying by offshore players on expectations that yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may encourage domestic authorities to act more aggressively to keep local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker. Traders note, BOK suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently of late to sell KRW amid concerns that undue KRW strength may hurt export-driven economic recovery. "Offshore players seem to think South Korea and Taiwan as the two most likely countries to move in harmony with Japan, as the two have been active in defending their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says local bank trader. Another trader notes, "domestic authorities are not suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  (yet today)." USD/KRW now up at 1,164.5 vs 1,157.2 low, vs 1,161.7 late yesterday in Seoul, with cap tipped at 1,170. JPY/KRW down sharply at 13.7138 vs 13.9026 yesterday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. is likely to provide implicit support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY, by withholding criticism, unless Japanese government attempts to weaken JPY over sustained period, says Peter Kenen, professor of economics at Princeton University and former Treasury consultant under several U.S. administrations. "This is aimed at warning people off and breaking the one-way bet that Japan has been experiencing," he adds, noting move, which he calls "both feasible and sensible," will likely be effective to counter upward drift in JPY. USD/JPY last at 84.85, well above intraday low at 82.85. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF is still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY via EBS, say 2 senior dealers at major banks. One says "the MOF continues to buy dollars against the yen ever since it first entered the market around 0135 GMT"; pair last at 84.90. Another adds "the MOF is piling up so many small lots, each worth about $10 million or so"; says pair may rise above 85.00 but offers there also heavy. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0345 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.3% at 446.75 on concerns over equity dilution after state media reports banks will issue VND80 trillion worth of new shares between now and year-end. Volume thin with 27 million shares valued at VND670 billion traded so far. Hanoi-based brokerage analyst says "many players are leaving the market in anticipation that stock prices, especially bank stocks, will fall unless government steps in to support the market by raising cash supply soon." Trader tips immediate support at 445. Among decliners, Vincom (VIC.VH) last down 4.3% at VND55,500, Kinh Bac City (KBC.VH) down 2.0% at VND33,800, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) down 0.8% at VND37,000. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0016ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM0000002