\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Shares Lower Midday Amid Rate Hike Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 1,562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 05:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend losses midday as banks pressured by continued concerns over prospect of rate hike from PBOC. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.6% at 2673.08, tipped to stay in tight 2650-2750 band rest of session. "This month may be the turning point for China's rising inflation, but investors still haven't ruled out the possibility of an interest rate hike," says Zhang Gang at Central China Securities. Says cautious mood may extend to mid-October, when September's macroeconomic data released, as few trading days left this month due to holidays. Bank of Communications (601328.SH) down 0.9% at CNY5.80, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Merchants Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (600036.SH) down 1.2% at CNY13.20. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.9% at 1196.96. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [Nikkei/Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  equity strategist Shun Maruyama says Japanese government's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market praiseworthy, but says some in market speculate long lasting effect may require bigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cooperation from other countries. That said, says even if yen's weakness is short-lived, government's decisive action sent message that it will act if/when necessary, and may take more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps from now on, which may discourage continued yen buying. Nikkei resistance seen at 9500 level. USD/JPY at 84.92, well up from sub-83 level seen earlier. Nikkei up 3.0% at 9576.13, up from midday close of 9470.31. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rebounds strongly on active USD buying by offshore players on expectations that yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may encourage domestic authorities to act more aggressively to keep local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker. Traders note, BOK suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently of late to sell KRW amid concerns that undue KRW strength may hurt export-driven economic recovery. "Offshore players seem to think South Korea and Taiwan as the two most likely countries to move in harmony with Japan, as the two have been active in defending their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says local bank trader. Another trader notes, "domestic authorities are not suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  (yet today)." USD/KRW now up at 1,164.5 vs 1,157.2 low, vs 1,161.7 late yesterday in Seoul, with cap tipped at 1,170. JPY/KRW down sharply at 13.7138 vs 13.9026 yesterday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. is likely to provide implicit support for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY, by withholding criticism, unless Japanese government attempts to weaken JPY over sustained period, says Peter Kenen, professor of economics at Princeton University and former Treasury consultant under several U.S. administrations. "This is aimed at warning people off and breaking the one-way bet that Japan has been experiencing," he adds, noting move, which he calls "both feasible and sensible," will likely be effective to counter upward drift in JPY. USD/JPY last at 84.85, well above intraday low at 82.85. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF is still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY via EBS, say 2 senior dealers at major banks. One says "the MOF continues to buy dollars against the yen ever since it first entered the market around 0135 GMT"; pair last at 84.90. Another adds "the MOF is piling up so many small lots, each worth about $10 million or so"; says pair may rise above 85.00 but offers there also heavy. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0345 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.3% at 446.75 on concerns over equity dilution after state media reports banks will issue VND80 trillion worth of new shares between now and year-end. Volume thin with 27 million shares valued at VND670 billion traded so far. Hanoi-based brokerage analyst says "many players are leaving the market in anticipation that stock prices, especially bank stocks, will fall unless government steps in to support the market by raising cash supply soon." Trader tips immediate support at 445. Among decliners, Vincom (VIC.VH) last down 4.3% at VND55,500, Kinh Bac City (KBC.VH) down 2.0% at VND33,800, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) down 0.8% at VND37,000. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0343 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher as European banks buy USD after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior trader at Singapore bank. Tips pair in 7.7650-7.7700 band near term. "The fall of yen after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  triggers some USD buying interest. I've spotted a Spanish bank, a German bank and a major U.K. bank buying the U.S. dollar near the 7.7660 level, but the purchases aren't sizable with US$20 million to US$30 million per deal," trader says. "Still, the lower USD/CNY fixing helps to cap USD/HKD's rise and I've spotted a local bank selling the U.S. dollar near the 7.7670 level," adds trader. Pair last at 7.7674 vs 7.7664 this morning, compared with 7.7673 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.8% at 914.98 on lingering concerns BOT may impose stronger measures to stem THB's rise. Foreigners' net selling yesterday also weighing market sentiment. Energy, petrochemical plays falling on concerns residents in Rayong will stage protest against recent court's ruling allowing 74 of 76 suspended projects at Map Ta Phut to proceed, while telecom stocks also under pressure after court set hearing for today on legal challenge to 3G mobile license auction, with state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2  seeking injunction. "A spate of negative factors is hitting the market," says Capital Nomura Securities strategist Chai Jirasevenupraphund. Support tipped at 908-905, resistance at 921. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) off 1.1% at THB279, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) flat at THB119, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.9% at THB92.75, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  off 1.3% at THB96, \uc2 Total Access Communication\uc2  (DTAC.TH) off 1.1% at THB45.25. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB higher at 30.82-30.84 vs 30.73-30.74 early, boosted by news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY, which prompts suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  suspected to have bought USD to push pair to intrasession high of 30.85, dealers say. "At first we thought the dollar/baht would be rangebound and biased downward. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and subsequently the suspected BOT \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed the direction," says one dealer. Some players jump on bandwagon covering shorts, another dealer says. Pair tipped in 30.75-30.90 range. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian skilled vacancies data, +0.1% on month in September vs 0.3% fall in August, reflect "super strength" of labor market, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  analyst Roland Randall says. Adds data - persisting near post financial crisis highs - consistent with continued strong employment growth, pushing unemployment rate down from 5.1% currently. Still, momentum in labor market "does appear to be slowing", so urgency for RBA to tighten rates sooner rather than later to combat labor market capacity constraints has eased recently. Mining state of Western Australia continues to account for an "outsized share" of job openings - 34% nationally, dominating demand for skilled tradespeople. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi turns lower, last down 0.1% at 1813.61, as news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY weighing on techs, capping gains in autos due to concerns over price competitiveness in export markets; "in particular, a weak yen is putting a pressure on IT parts shares such as Samsung SDI and Cheil Industries, while \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  won't rise further," says Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . Tips Kospi in 1810-1820 band. Adds domestic institutions' net selling of stocks worth KRW127 billion also weighs. Samsung SDI (006400.SE) down 6.1% at KRW154,500, Cheil Industries (001300.SE) off 7.5% at KRW99,000; among auto stocks, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +1.0% at KRW158,500, \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) +2.0% at KRW35,050. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open tad lower tracking overnight EUR gains vs USD, but news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may limit fall, says dealer with foreign bank; pair likely to open at 46.40 vs 46.46 late yesterday. "While euro was strong overnight, the dollar has clawed back after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, the rupee is unlikely to be affected as it is already a laggard amongst Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the RBI has stayed away from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," says dealer. Notes global stock markets also broadly flat which might cap gains in local unit. Tips pair in 46.30-46.60 band in session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR16.51 billion worth of stocks yesterday, 1-month NDFs quoting at 46.51/46.54. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2353ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0002s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
