\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/MYR Up On  \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX Intervention \highlight\uc2 ,Profit-Taking\b0\par\par\uc2 1,575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 05:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR at 3.1150 vs 3.1040 late yesterday, off earlier low of 3.1000, tracking rebound in USD vs other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market; dealers say news of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  coupled with suspected USD buying by Bank Negara at 3.1000 this week prompting market participants to take profits on MYR's recent appreciation. "Because of the suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by central banks in the region, players see little room for further upside for the ringgit in the near-term and are just content to take profits for now," dealer says; tips USD/MYR to remain in 3.1000-3.1200 range near-term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open tad higher by about 15-30 points helped by gains in most Asian markets and as mood remains upbeat after index managed to hold its intraday gains to end up for 6th-consecutive session yesterday, says local dealer. Adds, strong liquidity on back of sustained buying by foreign funds to further help trade. Overseas funds bought shares worth provisional INR16.51 billion Tuesday vs INR26.48 billion Monday. However, believes upside should be capped at 19,470--says technically markets look overbought--up over 6% in last 6 session and traders could start consolidating positions. Stretched valuations in most index stocks after recent rally, caution before RBI rate-setting meeting tomorrow to also cap upside. Sensex ended +0.7% at 19,346.96 Tuesday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures turn lower following news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which drags down KRW. "Following the yen's slip on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expectations of a weaker won are spurring concerns that foreign buying in won-based bonds may falter," says \uc2 SK Securities\uc2  analyst Yum Sang-hoon. Expects local bonds to show lackluster performance on unexpected KRW weakness. But adds "how effective Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be in keeping the yen weak remains in question, so I don't expect the negative impact on local bonds to last long." 3-year yield +2 bps at 3.41%, September futures down 12 ticks at 112.68 vs earlier 112.98 high. USD/KRW now up at 1,164.5 vs 1,161.7 yesterday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0314 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to open higher tracking lower U.S. Treasury yields but gains likely limited ahead of rate setting meeting Thursday, says dealer with private bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely to open in 99.05-99.15 band vs 99.01 at Tuesday's close. Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee Tuesday said despite lower August inflation there was no room for complacency and government will work towards lowering it further, use fiscal, monetary instruments as needed; comments will cement market expectation of a 25 basis points hike in rates by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "A quarter percentage hike is almost factored in. If the RBI decides to pause, the 10-year yield may rally to 7.90% (closed at 7.95%)," says dealer. Tips 2020 bond to be in 98.95-99.40 range in session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares sharply higher as local bourse plays catch-up after week-long holiday, dealers say; main index +3.2% at fresh all-time high of 3323.86. Market breadth positive with 108 gainers vs 17 decliners. "Foreigners continue to buy Indonesian stocks on expectations that Indonesia will get investment grade in foreseeable future," dealers say. Among gainers, \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) +8.7%; Telkom (TLKM.JK) +3.3%, \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) +7.7%; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +4.1%, and Bumi Resources (BUMI.JK) +5.6%. 3,250-3,350 now tipped for main index. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [Dow Jones] China attracts $7.6 billion of actual foreign direct investment in August, +1.38% on-year, vs +29.2% at $6.92 billion in July, +39.6% in June, Ministry of Commerce says. Economists say eased growth mostly due to high comparison base in August last year; also, foreign investment may has been affected following foreign companies' repeated complaints recently of inconsistent enforcement of rules, government procurement process biased toward local companies, insufficient protection for intellectual property rights. However add, monthly fluctuations not sufficient to fully portray trend. FDI in January-August period +18.06% on-year to $65.96 billion, vs +20.65% during January-July period; FDI totaled $58.35 billion in January-July. (li.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY strength may persist in weeks ahead despite MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly if U.S. Federal Reserve moves toward further easing, Treasury yields fall ahead, says Hideki Amikura, deputy general manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. "I'd give the yen about a 60% chance of staying strong." Says while USD/JPY could rise to around 85.00, substantial selling by Japan exporters there would likely weigh on pair. Adds, any USD/JPY buying by non-Japanese short-term investors in reaction to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlikely to continue. But says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at least, likely given USD/JPY a support at 83.00. "That could become the support for now, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducted after the breach of that level."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures pare gains after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken JPY. Contract hits intraday low of 141.74 after MOF move; last up 0.42 at 141.80. Despite Japan MOF move, JGBs expected to maintain solid bias in latter half of September, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets Japan senior strategist Maki Shimizu. "With the DPJ presidential race now over and fiscal concerns having already been digested in the bond market, players are willing to buy JGBs because prices have dipped excessively" ahead of DPJ election results yesterday. Adds, impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely limited on JGBs, as MOF action unlikely to reverse JPY's broad upward momentum. 10-year cash JGB yield last down 6.5 bps at 1.065%; tips 10-year cash JGB yield to reach 1.050% at end-September. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving higher, helped by impending requirements from some importers, particularly oil companies; pair last at 44.28 in moderate volume after settling at 44.26 late yesterday, but off early high at 44.35. "The early movement was probably caused by adjustments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixing rate (for dollar sales to importers)," says Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader; adds "we saw news about (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that hasn't affected the (local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market. (Our) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't in the market." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,268.70/oz, unchanged in Asia and holding most of yesterday's gains after metal ran to record high of $1,274.75. In a monthly update, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  says it expects in short-term for gold to hold current levels; says it'll take some time for turbulence of financial markets to calm, so safe haven demand to persist as possibility of a sovereign default or another economic contraction lingers. Moreover, jewellery consumption to further recover in coming months as household incomes continue to rise in line with stronger economic growth especially in China, India; adds mine output expected to be relatively flat and some producer de-hedging anticipated next 12 months. Forecasts spot gold to average $1,231 in 4Q, then rise a little to average $1,242 in 1Q and $1,240 in 2Q, before gradually sliding to lower levels. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD last down at 31.760 vs 31.815 yesterday, but off earlier low at 31.650, tracking weaker Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 2 local bank traders say; add importers buying at lows. 1 of them expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy USD late in session. Another trader says "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may encourage Asian central banks to follow suit." Tips pair in 31.680-31.820 band rest of session.(mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares higher with utilities, mining stocks leading gains after recent underperformance. Benchmark PSEi up 0.1% at 3973.76 in relatively heavy volume; resistance tipped at 4000, support at 3950. Market breadth positive with gainers outnumbering losers 62 to 50. "Money is being rotated (from banks and property plays) to mining and other laggard stocks. Investors are looking for new catalysts. We'll likely see the market move within the 3900-4000 range for the meantime," says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities. Digital Telecommunications (DGTL.PH) +6.1% at PHP1.56, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) +2.6% to PHP5.81, gold-copper producer \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH) +2.8% at PHP13.06. Among losers, Banco de Oro (BDO.PH) down 0.7% at PHP60.50, Ayala Land (ALI.PH) down 0.6% at PHP17.74. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2318ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
