\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: JGB Futures Pare Gains After MOF  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 04:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures pare gains after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken JPY. Contract hits intraday low of 141.74 after MOF move; last up 0.42 at 141.80. Despite Japan MOF move, JGBs expected to maintain solid bias in latter half of September, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets Japan senior strategist Maki Shimizu. "With the DPJ presidential race now over and fiscal concerns having already been digested in the bond market, players are willing to buy JGBs because prices have dipped excessively" ahead of DPJ election results yesterday. Adds, impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely limited on JGBs, as MOF action unlikely to reverse JPY's broad upward momentum. 10-year cash JGB yield last down 6.5 bps at 1.065%; tips 10-year cash JGB yield to reach 1.050% at end-September. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving higher, helped by impending requirements from some importers, particularly oil companies; pair last at 44.28 in moderate volume after settling at 44.26 late yesterday, but off early high at 44.35. "The early movement was probably caused by adjustments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixing rate (for dollar sales to importers)," says Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader; adds "we saw news about (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that hasn't affected the (local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market. (Our) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't in the market." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,268.70/oz, unchanged in Asia and holding most of yesterday's gains after metal ran to record high of $1,274.75. In a monthly update, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  says it expects in short-term for gold to hold current levels; says it'll take some time for turbulence of financial markets to calm, so safe haven demand to persist as possibility of a sovereign default or another economic contraction lingers. Moreover, jewellery consumption to further recover in coming months as household incomes continue to rise in line with stronger economic growth especially in China, India; adds mine output expected to be relatively flat and some producer de-hedging anticipated next 12 months. Forecasts spot gold to average $1,231 in 4Q, then rise a little to average $1,242 in 1Q and $1,240 in 2Q, before gradually sliding to lower levels. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD last down at 31.760 vs 31.815 yesterday, but off earlier low at 31.650, tracking weaker Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after news of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 2 local bank traders say; add importers buying at lows. 1 of them expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy USD late in session. Another trader says "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may encourage Asian central banks to follow suit." Tips pair in 31.680-31.820 band rest of session.(mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares higher with utilities, mining stocks leading gains after recent underperformance. Benchmark PSEi up 0.1% at 3973.76 in relatively heavy volume; resistance tipped at 4000, support at 3950. Market breadth positive with gainers outnumbering losers 62 to 50. "Money is being rotated (from banks and property plays) to mining and other laggard stocks. Investors are looking for new catalysts. We'll likely see the market move within the 3900-4000 range for the meantime," says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities. Digital Telecommunications (DGTL.PH) +6.1% at PHP1.56, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) +2.6% to PHP5.81, gold-copper producer \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH) +2.8% at PHP13.06. Among losers, Banco de Oro (BDO.PH) down 0.7% at PHP60.50, Ayala Land (ALI.PH) down 0.6% at PHP17.74. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] A wide correction higher in USD/Asians looking more likely after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier today, says Richard Yetsenga, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Notes bullishness on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lifted all boats, but concerns on global economic prospects should drive investors to be more discriminating, especially now that Japan has reinforced message that Asian policymakers remain focused on keeping local markets under control. But adds it remains to be seen whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be successful in actually reversing downward trend for USD/JPY. USD/JPY last 84.48, well above intraday low at 82.85. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.8% at 9470.31 midday, well off intraday low of 9199.08 after opening lower, following sharp rise in USD/JPY from sub-83 level to over 84 (now 84.46) on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Gains driven primarily by futures buying by foreign funds, traders say. "The timing came as a surprise for players who had been betting on the yen to rise and stocks to fall" after PM Kan won ruling Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election on Tuesday, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. But adds Nikkei resistance pegged around 9500 with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact expected to be temporary amid speculation U.S. Fed may further ease monetary policy in near future. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like dressing a flesh wound" (when Kan needs to face more serious problem of deflation), he says. "This (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not a fundamental solution." 32/33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 3.5% at Y3,000, leading automakers, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 2.4% at Y2,553, Canon (7751.TO) up 2.0% at Y3,840. Advantest (6857.TO) down 0.1% at Y1,649 after \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  rating cut. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to rebound after yesterday's sharp fall. "The (Thai) stock market plunged yesterday on concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may introduce tough measures to stem the baht's rise. But they haven't done anything so far. It was overreacting," says analyst with Ayudhya Securities Adisak Phupiphathirungul. However, rebound likely capped as market's valuation less attractive following recent rally, he adds. SET index has risen 25.4% from market close on December 30 last year. Telecom stocks in focus after court accepted state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2 's petition seeking injunction against 3G mobile license auction scheduled to start next week. Resistance tipped at 930, support at 920. SET index yesterday ends down 1.7% at 921.39. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY rallies to highest level in nearly 6 weeks on news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders say. Still, Richard Grace, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , says move more about a weaker JPY; "if they're buying dollars, it makes it difficult for the Aussie to lift higher in that environment because there is a bid for U.S. dollars in the market. Any gains in Aussie/Yen come through in weakness for Yen, not really strength in Aussie," says Grace. AUD/JPY last up to 79.20 from around 77.95 just prior to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  news, highest point since early August. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF continues "aggressive" USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY above 84.00 levels, say two senior dealers at major banks in Asia; "the amount is overwhelming, and my impression is Japan may want to push the dollar above 85.00 at least," one says. Pair last at 84.30. (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/JPY stronger alongside other yen crosses after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen, says BNZ strategist Mike Jones; NZD/JPY "jumped from around 60.80 to 61.80; I guess the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had some success to date as the yen has weakened across the board including against the kiwi dollar." Cross last 61.86; adds this is highest level since August 11. Says USD/JPY has had biggest gain; "we might see the market now a little bit more cautious that the BOJ is backing up its rhetoric and we might see the yen continue its losses in the next 24 hours." Tips NZD/JPY support at 59.80 with no resistance until 63.45. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex last +0.2% at 8148.86, KGI analyst Marc-Andre Gilbert says trading likely in tight 50-point range as investors rotate into laggards in various sectors. "But continued buying from foreign investors as well as local investment trusts and advisory firms, who are said to benefit from large sums of money to manage on the part of government funds, will all be looking to scoop up shares on any weakness." Tips techs to remain major pointer of overall market; HTC (2498.TW) down 2.1% at NT$739.00 on profit taking, after a 17% gain in last 4 sessions, Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.4% at NT$114.50, TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.5% at NT$60.70%. Also says to watch out for financial stocks "for an indication on the timing of the next move up." (aries.poon@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2255ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
