\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Philippine Shares +0.1%; Mining Stocks Lead\b0\par\par\uc2 1,522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 04:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares higher with utilities, mining stocks leading gains after recent underperformance. Benchmark PSEi up 0.1% at 3973.76 in relatively heavy volume; resistance tipped at 4000, support at 3950. Market breadth positive with gainers outnumbering losers 62 to 50. "Money is being rotated (from banks and property plays) to mining and other laggard stocks. Investors are looking for new catalysts. We'll likely see the market move within the 3900-4000 range for the meantime," says Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities. Digital Telecommunications (DGTL.PH) +6.1% at PHP1.56, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) +2.6% to PHP5.81, gold-copper producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philex Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PX.PH) +2.8% at PHP13.06. Among losers, Banco de Oro (BDO.PH) down 0.7% at PHP60.50, Ayala Land (ALI.PH) down 0.6% at PHP17.74. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] A wide correction higher in USD/Asians looking more likely after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier today, says Richard Yetsenga, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Notes bullishness on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lifted all boats, but concerns on global economic prospects should drive investors to be more discriminating, especially now that Japan has reinforced message that Asian policymakers remain focused on keeping local markets under control. But adds it remains to be seen whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be successful in actually reversing downward trend for USD/JPY. USD/JPY last 84.48, well above intraday low at 82.85. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.8% at 9470.31 midday, well off intraday low of 9199.08 after opening lower, following sharp rise in USD/JPY from sub-83 level to over 84 (now 84.46) on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Gains driven primarily by futures buying by foreign funds, traders say. "The timing came as a surprise for players who had been betting on the yen to rise and stocks to fall" after PM Kan won ruling Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election on Tuesday, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. But adds Nikkei resistance pegged around 9500 with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact expected to be temporary amid speculation U.S. Fed may further ease monetary policy in near future. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like dressing a flesh wound" (when Kan needs to face more serious problem of deflation), he says. "This (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not a fundamental solution." 32/33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 3.5% at Y3,000, leading automakers, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 2.4% at Y2,553, Canon (7751.TO) up 2.0% at Y3,840. Advantest (6857.TO) down 0.1% at Y1,649 after \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  rating cut. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to rebound after yesterday's sharp fall. "The (Thai) stock market plunged yesterday on concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may introduce tough measures to stem the baht's rise. But they haven't done anything so far. It was overreacting," says analyst with Ayudhya Securities Adisak Phupiphathirungul. However, rebound likely capped as market's valuation less attractive following recent rally, he adds. SET index has risen 25.4% from market close on December 30 last year. Telecom stocks in focus after court accepted state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2 's petition seeking injunction against 3G mobile license auction scheduled to start next week. Resistance tipped at 930, support at 920. SET index yesterday ends down 1.7% at 921.39. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY rallies to highest level in nearly 6 weeks on news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders say. Still, Richard Grace, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , says move more about a weaker JPY; "if they're buying dollars, it makes it difficult for the Aussie to lift higher in that environment because there is a bid for U.S. dollars in the market. Any gains in Aussie/Yen come through in weakness for Yen, not really strength in Aussie," says Grace. AUD/JPY last up to 79.20 from around 77.95 just prior to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  news, highest point since early August. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF continues "aggressive" USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY above 84.00 levels, say two senior dealers at major banks in Asia; "the amount is overwhelming, and my impression is Japan may want to push the dollar above 85.00 at least," one says. Pair last at 84.30. (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/JPY stronger alongside other yen crosses after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen, says BNZ strategist Mike Jones; NZD/JPY "jumped from around 60.80 to 61.80; I guess the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had some success to date as the yen has weakened across the board including against the kiwi dollar." Cross last 61.86; adds this is highest level since August 11. Says USD/JPY has had biggest gain; "we might see the market now a little bit more cautious that the BOJ is backing up its rhetoric and we might see the yen continue its losses in the next 24 hours." Tips NZD/JPY support at 59.80 with no resistance until 63.45. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex last +0.2% at 8148.86, KGI analyst Marc-Andre Gilbert says trading likely in tight 50-point range as investors rotate into laggards in various sectors. "But continued buying from foreign investors as well as local investment trusts and advisory firms, who are said to benefit from large sums of money to manage on the part of government funds, will all be looking to scoop up shares on any weakness." Tips techs to remain major pointer of overall market; HTC (2498.TW) down 2.1% at NT$739.00 on profit taking, after a 17% gain in last 4 sessions, Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.4% at NT$114.50, TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.5% at NT$60.70%. Also says to watch out for financial stocks "for an indication on the timing of the next move up." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +0.2% at 21,737.88, up for 5th consecutive session, but it looks hard to test 22,000 for now, as index may witness consolidation near term after its recent rise. Jamie Coutts at BGC Securities says technicals looking tired after recent rise (+5.6% month-to-yesterday), with index failing to stay above 21,800 after its initial breakthrough earlier today (early high at 21,819.95). HKEx (0388.HK) down 0.1% at HK$137.10, as further gains in HSI likely capped. Some HK property stocks down on profit taking, with \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) down 0.4% at HK$14.44, \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) down 0.3% at HK$37.10. Volume brisk at HK$9.12 billion, inflated by fundraising plans from a number of companies, including China Resouces Gas (1193.HK) with volume at HK$2.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] While USD/JPY has pushed up to 84.56 on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , senior dealers at major banks in Tokyo say they don't expect rise will last with exporters aggressively offering over 84.00 mark; "once this event is over, we'll see the dollar below 83.50 again soon," says one. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW, USD/TWD, USD/CNY, USD/SGD moving up after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, causing jump in USD/JPY, as markets brace for reaction from other Asian central banks. "A weakening of the yen is a negative for emerging market Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Dariusz Kowalczyk, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , noting that KRW, TWD at biggest risk of retracement given those economies' export mix, fairly similar to Japan. Reckons that China may also react by slowing the CNY appreciation pace -- USD/CNY already inching higher after yet another low fixing by PBOC. USD/CNY last at intraday high at 6.7374, well above intraday low prior to Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at 6.7300. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0148 GMT mischaracterized USD/TWD's closing low yesterday. The correct version is as follows:) USD/TWD lower at 31.700 vs over 5-week closing low 31.815 yesterday, marking 6th-straight session of losses, as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted by lower USD/CNY fixing, says local bank trader. "If the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain biased up, the pair is likely to try the key 31.500 or even 31.300 supports in the near term," he says. Tips pair in wide 31.600-31.800 band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2237ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
