\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares Likely Up; Telecom Stocks In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 1,457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 04:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to rebound after yesterday's sharp fall. "The (Thai) stock market plunged yesterday on concerns that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may introduce tough measures to stem the baht's rise. But they haven't done anything so far. It was overreacting," says analyst with Ayudhya Securities Adisak Phupiphathirungul. However, rebound likely capped as market's valuation less attractive following recent rally, he adds. SET index has risen 25.4% from market close on December 30 last year. Telecom stocks in focus after court accepted state-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 CAT Telecom\f28 \uc2 \f2 's petition seeking injunction against 3G mobile license auction scheduled to start next week. Resistance tipped at 930, support at 920. SET index yesterday ends down 1.7% at 921.39. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY rallies to highest level in nearly 6 weeks on news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders say. Still, Richard Grace, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , says move more about a weaker JPY; "if they're buying dollars, it makes it difficult for the Aussie to lift higher in that environment because there is a bid for U.S. dollars in the market. Any gains in Aussie/Yen come through in weakness for Yen, not really strength in Aussie," says Grace. AUD/JPY last up to 79.20 from around 77.95 just prior to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  news, highest point since early August. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF continues "aggressive" USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY above 84.00 levels, say two senior dealers at major banks in Asia; "the amount is overwhelming, and my impression is Japan may want to push the dollar above 85.00 at least," one says. Pair last at 84.30. (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/JPY stronger alongside other yen crosses after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen, says BNZ strategist Mike Jones; NZD/JPY "jumped from around 60.80 to 61.80; I guess the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had some success to date as the yen has weakened across the board including against the kiwi dollar." Cross last 61.86; adds this is highest level since August 11. Says USD/JPY has had biggest gain; "we might see the market now a little bit more cautious that the BOJ is backing up its rhetoric and we might see the yen continue its losses in the next 24 hours." Tips NZD/JPY support at 59.80 with no resistance until 63.45. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex last +0.2% at 8148.86, KGI analyst Marc-Andre Gilbert says trading likely in tight 50-point range as investors rotate into laggards in various sectors. "But continued buying from foreign investors as well as local investment trusts and advisory firms, who are said to benefit from large sums of money to manage on the part of government funds, will all be looking to scoop up shares on any weakness." Tips techs to remain major pointer of overall market; HTC (2498.TW) down 2.1% at NT$739.00 on profit taking, after a 17% gain in last 4 sessions, Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.4% at NT$114.50, TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.5% at NT$60.70%. Also says to watch out for financial stocks "for an indication on the timing of the next move up." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +0.2% at 21,737.88, up for 5th consecutive session, but it looks hard to test 22,000 for now, as index may witness consolidation near term after its recent rise. Jamie Coutts at BGC Securities says technicals looking tired after recent rise (+5.6% month-to-yesterday), with index failing to stay above 21,800 after its initial breakthrough earlier today (early high at 21,819.95). HKEx (0388.HK) down 0.1% at HK$137.10, as further gains in HSI likely capped. Some HK property stocks down on profit taking, with \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) down 0.4% at HK$14.44, \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) down 0.3% at HK$37.10. Volume brisk at HK$9.12 billion, inflated by fundraising plans from a number of companies, including China Resouces Gas (1193.HK) with volume at HK$2.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] While USD/JPY has pushed up to 84.56 on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , senior dealers at major banks in Tokyo say they don't expect rise will last with exporters aggressively offering over 84.00 mark; "once this event is over, we'll see the dollar below 83.50 again soon," says one. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW, USD/TWD, USD/CNY, USD/SGD moving up after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, causing jump in USD/JPY, as markets brace for reaction from other Asian central banks. "A weakening of the yen is a negative for emerging market Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Dariusz Kowalczyk, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , noting that KRW, TWD at biggest risk of retracement given those economies' export mix, fairly similar to Japan. Reckons that China may also react by slowing the CNY appreciation pace -- USD/CNY already inching higher after yet another low fixing by PBOC. USD/CNY last at intraday high at 6.7374, well above intraday low prior to Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at 6.7300. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0148 GMT mischaracterized USD/TWD's closing low yesterday. The correct version is as follows:) USD/TWD lower at 31.700 vs over 5-week closing low 31.815 yesterday, marking 6th-straight session of losses, as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted by lower USD/CNY fixing, says local bank trader. "If the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain biased up, the pair is likely to try the key 31.500 or even 31.300 supports in the near term," he says. Tips pair in wide 31.600-31.800 band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] ("MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Rises Further After BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 " at 0210 GMT, misstated the 4 month high. The correct version follows:) Australia's S&P/ASX 200 up 0.6% at 4-month high of 4653.5 after BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY, causing traders to expect gains in risk assets, as JPY unwinds some of its recent safe haven strength. "The biggest beneficiary will be Australian big four banks who have a 100% correlation with the AUD/JPY cross rate," says Southern Cross Equities director Charlie Aitken, who predicted BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his morning newsletter. Index breaks through May 13 high at 4652.8, leaving index heading for next Fibonacci target at 4710 (61.8% retracement of April/June fall), according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Should USD/JPY close in NY today above 85.00 it could provide impetus for follow through dollar strength after today's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus analyst Mark Cranfield. On charts USD/JPY has been in tight downward sloping channel since its July peak at 89.15; top of channel currently at 84.72, hence move above 85.00 could bring in fresh dollar bulls. Should USD/JPY break its short-term down trend, first target on charts is 85.95 (55-day moving average), above which late July high at 88.11. Failure to breach 85.00 today may leave USD/JPY vulnerable to new lows in days ahead.(mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off earlier 1,157.2 low, tracking sharp rebound in USD/JPY following Japanese authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. USD/KRW at 1,160.3 vs 1,161.7 late yesterday in Seoul. Analysts say USD/JPY gain triggering broad USD gains vs other majors such as EUR, GBP, AUD in Asian trade. "Anticipation that dollar-buying by Japanese authorities could lead to a broadly stronger dollar," is boosting USD/KRW as well, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. Expects local pair to see-saw around 1,160 rest of session, within 1,157-1,163 range. Woori Futures analyst Byeon Ji-young, meanwhile, notes, JPY weakness on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could trigger market interest in taking JPY/KRW shorts, cap upside for USD/KRW later. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2233ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0001u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
