\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan Shares +0.2%; Rotation Among Laggards\b0\par\par\uc2 1,467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 04:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex last +0.2% at 8148.86, KGI analyst Marc-Andre Gilbert says trading likely in tight 50-point range as investors rotate into laggards in various sectors. "But continued buying from foreign investors as well as local investment trusts and advisory firms, who are said to benefit from large sums of money to manage on the part of government funds, will all be looking to scoop up shares on any weakness." Tips techs to remain major pointer of overall market; HTC (2498.TW) down 2.1% at NT$739.00 on profit taking, after a 17% gain in last 4 sessions, Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 0.4% at NT$114.50, TSMC (2330.TW) down 0.5% at NT$60.70%. Also says to watch out for financial stocks "for an indication on the timing of the next move up." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +0.2% at 21,737.88, up for 5th consecutive session, but it looks hard to test 22,000 for now, as index may witness consolidation near term after its recent rise. Jamie Coutts at BGC Securities says technicals looking tired after recent rise (+5.6% month-to-yesterday), with index failing to stay above 21,800 after its initial breakthrough earlier today (early high at 21,819.95). HKEx (0388.HK) down 0.1% at HK$137.10, as further gains in HSI likely capped. Some HK property stocks down on profit taking, with \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) down 0.4% at HK$14.44, \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) down 0.3% at HK$37.10. Volume brisk at HK$9.12 billion, inflated by fundraising plans from a number of companies, including China Resouces Gas (1193.HK) with volume at HK$2.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] While USD/JPY has pushed up to 84.56 on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , senior dealers at major banks in Tokyo say they don't expect rise will last with exporters aggressively offering over 84.00 mark; "once this event is over, we'll see the dollar below 83.50 again soon," says one. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW, USD/TWD, USD/CNY, USD/SGD moving up after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, causing jump in USD/JPY, as markets brace for reaction from other Asian central banks. "A weakening of the yen is a negative for emerging market Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Dariusz Kowalczyk, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , noting that KRW, TWD at biggest risk of retracement given those economies' export mix, fairly similar to Japan. Reckons that China may also react by slowing the CNY appreciation pace -- USD/CNY already inching higher after yet another low fixing by PBOC. USD/CNY last at intraday high at 6.7374, well above intraday low prior to Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at 6.7300. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0148 GMT mischaracterized USD/TWD's closing low yesterday. The correct version is as follows:) USD/TWD lower at 31.700 vs over 5-week closing low 31.815 yesterday, marking 6th-straight session of losses, as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted by lower USD/CNY fixing, says local bank trader. "If the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain biased up, the pair is likely to try the key 31.500 or even 31.300 supports in the near term," he says. Tips pair in wide 31.600-31.800 band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] ("MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Rises Further After BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 " at 0210 GMT, misstated the 4 month high. The correct version follows:) Australia's S&P/ASX 200 up 0.6% at 4-month high of 4653.5 after BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY, causing traders to expect gains in risk assets, as JPY unwinds some of its recent safe haven strength. "The biggest beneficiary will be Australian big four banks who have a 100% correlation with the AUD/JPY cross rate," says Southern Cross Equities director Charlie Aitken, who predicted BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his morning newsletter. Index breaks through May 13 high at 4652.8, leaving index heading for next Fibonacci target at 4710 (61.8% retracement of April/June fall), according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Should USD/JPY close in NY today above 85.00 it could provide impetus for follow through dollar strength after today's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus analyst Mark Cranfield. On charts USD/JPY has been in tight downward sloping channel since its July peak at 89.15; top of channel currently at 84.72, hence move above 85.00 could bring in fresh dollar bulls. Should USD/JPY break its short-term down trend, first target on charts is 85.95 (55-day moving average), above which late July high at 88.11. Failure to breach 85.00 today may leave USD/JPY vulnerable to new lows in days ahead.(mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off earlier 1,157.2 low, tracking sharp rebound in USD/JPY following Japanese authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. USD/KRW at 1,160.3 vs 1,161.7 late yesterday in Seoul. Analysts say USD/JPY gain triggering broad USD gains vs other majors such as EUR, GBP, AUD in Asian trade. "Anticipation that dollar-buying by Japanese authorities could lead to a broadly stronger dollar," is boosting USD/KRW as well, says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. Expects local pair to see-saw around 1,160 rest of session, within 1,157-1,163 range. Woori Futures analyst Byeon Ji-young, meanwhile, notes, JPY weakness on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could trigger market interest in taking JPY/KRW shorts, cap upside for USD/KRW later. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 up 0.6% at 4653.5, hits fresh 4-month high of 4651.8 after BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY, causing traders to expect gains in risk assets, as JPY unwinds some of its recent safe haven strength. "The biggest beneficiary will be Australian big four banks who have a 100% correlation with the AUD/JPY cross rate," says Southern Cross Equities director Charlie Aitken, who predicted BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his morning newsletter. Index breaks through May 13 high at 4652.8, leaving index heading for next Fibonacci target at 4710 (61.8% retracement of April/June fall), according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY, EUR/JPY, JPY crosses jump after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. While many market participants had speculated that Kan win Tuesday in DPJ party president election against Ozawa, who was viewed as more vocal proponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would lessen likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , senior dealer at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank says "even from when he was finance minister, it was clear Kan wanted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Two traders say MOF was spotted selling Y200 billion To Y300 billion vs USD. USD/JPY hits 84.30 vs earlier low of 82.87, while EUR/JPY jumps to 109.35 from 107.74 earlier; AUD/JPY also sharply higher while EUR/USD has moved down. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up amid easing concerns over inflation after NDRC vice director Zhang Xiaoqiang said yesterday China can still achieve its targeted annual CPI increase of around 3.0% despite CPI rising by near 2-year high in August. "Zhang's comment strengthens expectations Beijing won't introduce interest rate hikes this year as the inflation growth will slow down in the coming months," says Shenzhen-based trader with local fund. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.89 vs 126.87 last close, tipped to test 127.0 by Friday. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 futures jump 1.7% to 9410 vs intraday low of 9140 on news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, traders say. "It caught us by surprise," says Japanese brokerage manager, adding futures buying also driving up cash market (Nikkei up 1.8% midday). Fund manager at U.S. asset management firm says investors had been speculating government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon as USD/JPY was trading below 83 this morning. Adds both Nikkei and Nikkei futures may trade above 9500 in coming days. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2227ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
