\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Up 0.4%; Sets 4-Month High\b0\par\par\uc2 1,634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 02:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.4% at 4643.1, sets fresh 4-month high of 4650.5 as materials lead broad-based gains after stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data last night. Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 2.8%, providing the biggest boost to the market, after spot gold hit a fresh record high of US$1,274.50. Elsewhere in materials, gains are muted, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 0.3%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 0.1%. Banks are mixed. NAB (NAB.AU) trading halted until 0100 GMT after dropping its bid for AXA APH (AXA.AU). Woodside (WPL.AU) up 1.0% despite weaker oil prices, while Leighton (LEI.AU) up 1.0%, \uc2 Transurban\uc2  (TCL.AU) up 1.8%. Consumer staples underpinning the market, with Woolworths (WOW.AU) up 0.5%, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 0.4%. S&P/ASX 200 outperforming S&P 500 by about 0.5% and S&P 500 futures are slightly weaker, so S&P/ASX 200 might struggle from here. "There's probably a fair chance we might have seen the high already," says Macquarie Private Wealth Investment Adviser Shannon Briggs. "It was good to see some confirmation that the U.S. economy is improving and that might prevent too much profit taking after a really good run in equities but we will want to see the rest of the U.S. data this week." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 0.2% at 9279.76 as investors watch closely whether PM Naoto Kan, who won Democratic Party of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (DPJ) presidential election Tuesday, will carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem USD/JPY fall after pair below 83 overnight (now 83.12). Analysts say Nikkei to trade between 9100-9300 for today. "The key themes (concerning Kan) thus far have been policy delay, verbal warnings (over yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), and (general) weakness," says Meiwa Securities senior market analyst Masayoshi Yano. "If he (Kan) can carry out unilateral (yen) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that would serve as a shot in the arm to the markets," he says, noting caution over such possible action helping stemming slide in exporter shares. 25/33 Topix subindexes higher; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 0.6% at Y2,916. Chip-related shares underperforming on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's rating cuts; Advantest (6857.TO) down 2.5% at Y1,608, \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) down 1.4% at Y4,170. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) off 0.9% at Y2,471 on firmer yen. \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  (5713.TO) up 0.9% at Y1,238 after gold hit fresh record high overnight. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 last +0.2% at 3203.40, likely to hold steady as market consolidates large gains over last week, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Nigel Scott; "the market has had a couple of 100-point rallies (in the last month)...so the market will be looking to consolidate on the current market position." Says Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) down 0.2% at NZ$8.50, Steel & Tube (STU.NZ) down 2.0% at NZ$2.52, \uc2 Mainfreight\uc2  (MFT.NZ) down 0.8% at NZ$7.18 after strong rally in 10 days following Christchurch earthquake. Adds Port of Tauranga (POT.NZ) down 2.5% at NZ$6.70 as it went ex-dividend this morning. \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  +3.6% at NZ$0.58 after announcing it had developed new compressor technology for fridges that could save 30% of power usage. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to rise tracking overnight gains in Treasurys, says trader with foreign bank. "We are seeing a good amount of inflows as investors still remain biased toward safe-haven instruments," trader says. Tips yields to fall between 2-3 bps. Yesterday, 2-year bond yield ended flat at 0.49%, while 10-year fell 4 bps to close at 2.13%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian consumer sentiment fell 5% in September from August, reversing a strong rise in the previous month, and casting doubt over an emerging view that household demand is rebounding. Confidence index fell to 113.2 points in September in seasonally adjusted terms from 119.2 points in August, said \uc2 Westpac\uc2  in its monthly survey. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  chief economist Bill Evans says result is surprising as it comes after a period of steady interest rates, solid employment data, and a rising AUD. Adds it suggests the jump in August may have been overstated. Consumers appear concerned about the economic outlook most. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,160 support on broadly weaker USD overnight, while Kospi +0.5%; pair now at 1,157.5 vs 1,161.7 late yesterday. Traders put key support at 1,155, where market likely on watch for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities; "players seem a bit uncomfortable about pushing the pair further down as it is nearing its previous low (at 1,155). But exporters are bound to sell" before national holiday next week, says local bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures open sharply higher, last up 0.53 at 141.91 points, as PM Kan's victory Tuesday in ruling party DPJ's leadership ballot eases fears of aggressive fiscal expansion, at least near term; smooth 20-, 30-year JGB auctions also adding to upside pressure. Next tender for long-term JGBs on October 7, for 10-year bonds, "meaning we won't have any fresh supply of long-term bonds until then" so demand will become stronger than supply, positive for JGBs, says Mizuho Securities senior market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Tips futures in 141.40-142.10 range today, 10-year yield in 1.030%-1.130% band, last down 7 bps at 1.060%. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower at 3.1000 vs 3.1040 late yesterday, tracking broad USD weakness; dealers say pair likely to continue testing 3.1000 today, breach of this key support would mark fresh 13-year low. "It remains to be seen if the dollar/ringgit will fall below 3.1000 because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely believed to have been supporting the dollar at that level over the past 2 days," dealer says. Tips pair to stay in 3.0850-3.1100 near term. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may fall, tracking weaker U.S. stocks overnight, profit-taking ahead of national holiday tomorrow. KLCI rose a 3rd straight session yesterday to end +1.2% at 1474.44, highest since January 2008. "Profit-taking will probably set-in, but that will not change the outlook for the market, which remains on track to test the major 1500 psychological resistance in the coming weeks," says dealer. KLCI tipped to move in 1465-1475 range today. Blue chips such as Genting (3182.KU), Public Bank (1295.KU), among yesterday's leading gainers, may retreat.(benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may be off to sluggish start after mixed Wall Street session. Having hit year-to-date peak earlier this week, daily chart indicates STI may not mark another 2010 record in near term, with resistance at current 3068 high, then lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008; support at week's low of 3026. Index fell 0.6% to 3048.65 yesterday. "It would be a marvel if it goes up to 3200 in a straight line in the remaining three and a half months of 2010. The least to be expected would be mild to moderate falls to test support around 3000 and 2950," says AmFraser Securities strategist Najeeb Jarhom. Ascott Residence (A68U.SG) may fall as REIT firms up dilutive equity fund-raising exercise for purchase of 28 properties from \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2 's (C31.SG) Ascott Group; it closed flat at S$1.15. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,161.7 late yesterday in Seoul, tracking broad USD weakness overnight, likely USD selling by exporters. Pair to test 1,160 support; but traders note attempt at breaching support likely challenged by possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, possible USD buying by companies relating to recent M&A deals; still unlikely to reverse pair's downward trend. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,152-1,164 range for day, with moderate downward bias. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ likely to leave official cash rate at 3.0% Thursday due to damaged consumer confidence from earthquake, soft economic data, says Matthew Circosta, economist for \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics; "The hit to consumer confidence, business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and household wealth from New Zealand's devastating earthquake has all but dashed chances of a third straight interest rate increase by the Reserve Bank of New Zealand this week." Adds, after September meeting, expects rising wage, price pressure from recovery to force \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain tightening bias; "We still expect stimulus measures to be withdrawn this year, with the policy rate now forecast to end 2010 at 3.5%, compared with 3.75% before the quake." Says in year ahead, quake reconstruction will add to business investment fueling already-upbeat outlook for the economy; "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not stay on the sidelines for too long." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 2048ET ]\