\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Threatens  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Yen Hits New 15-Year High\b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 05:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Combines finance minister's and economy minister's remarks; adds background)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened again Tuesday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after the yen pushed to a fresh 15-year high against the dollar, keeping traders on their toes ahead of a ruling party leadership election later in the day that could trigger more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said the outcome of the Democratic Party of Japan leadership race--in which Prime Minister Naoto Kan is locked in a tight battle with challenger Ichiro Ozawa--would have no bearing on the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance.\par \par \uc2 Few traders expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in more than six years at current levels. But any abrupt lurch higher would increase the risk of yen-selling by the authorities to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's relentless rise.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo fears a stronger yen could intensify deflation, hurt exporters and drive Japanese manufacturers offshore to lower production costs and keep up with rivals.\par \par \uc2 "Regardless of the presidential election, we will not create any political vacuum, and in accordance with our already-established policy, we will take decisive steps including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary," Noda said at a news conference.\par \par \uc2 The government won't tolerate quick, sharp rises or a prolonged upward trend in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could destabilize Japan's economy, he said.\par \par \uc2 Satoshi Arai, minister of state for economic and fiscal policy, also voiced concern about the yen's continued appreciation, and kept pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to work closely with the government on the matter.\par \par \uc2 "Even though we have been saying we would take decisive action if necessary, the yen is still rising. That means we will need to work harder with the Bank of Japan" to fight the strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Satoshi Arai, minister of state for economic and fiscal policy, at a regular press conference.\par \par \uc2 "Whoever wins in today's leadership race, I think the government's current approach to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is the best way," he said. "If Ozawa wins, small policy steps could change, but I believe our basic policy will remain unchanged, including our close cooperation with the BOJ."\par \par \uc2 Ozawa, in an unusual move for politicians in a party race, has repeatedly called for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y83.46 at 0240 GMT, recovering from an earlier dip to Y83.25.\par \par \uc2 Noda, reiterating a government statement last week that accompanied its economic stimulus package, said: "We can't overlook any rapid acceleration or any continuation of the yen's appreciation as it could have adverse effects on economic and financial conditions."\par \par \uc2 Noda declined to discuss comments made Friday by Prime Minister Kan, which raised speculation that Tokyo is in talk with the U.S. and Europe to gain permission for solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 Kan said he is asking other countries "not to say negative things in case Japan takes some kind of action."\par \par \uc2 It is unusual for the prime minister to publicly talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , let alone behind-the-door negotiations with foreign governments over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 The results of the DPJ presidential election are due later in the in Tokyo afternoon. Analysts say it is a tough call, although many reckon Kan has the lead at this point.\par \par \uc2 Because it controls the powerful Lower House of Parliament, the DPJ determines the country's prime minister. By convention, the ruling party's leader gets the top job, which must be put to a vote in Parliament.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 09-13-10 2331ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020100914e69e0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
