\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Mostly Lower; DJIA Snaps Four-Session Winning Streak\b0\par\par\uc2 2,681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 00:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10526.49  -17.64  -0.17% \par Nasdaq     2289.77   +4.06  +0.18% \par S&P 500    1121.10   -0.80  -0.07% \par FTSE 100   5567.41   +1.88  +0.03% \par Xetra DAX  6275.41  +13.73  +0.22% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 24/32  +2/32 0.495% -4.0BP \par 5Y Tsy   99 6/32 +14/32 1.422% -9.2BP \par 10Y Tsy 99 21/32 +23/32 2.663% -8.3BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.08-13     -0.68% \par Range         83.75-82.93 \par EUR/USD       1.2993-95    +0.92% \par Range         1.3033-1.2830 \par AUD/USD       0.9405-09    +0.53% \par Range         0.9458-0.9314 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    12.10-12.40% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.75-11.25% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    11.60-12.30% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,271.70  +$24.60 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,246.00-$1,276.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $76.80    -$0.39 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks were mostly slightly lower Tuesday as investors worried about the economy despite better-than-expected signs from the retail sector that boosted \f28 \uc2 \f2 Best Buy\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.C. Penney\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kroger\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average declined 17.64 points, or 0.17%, to 10526.49, its first drop in five sessions. The measure's financial components were among its weakest, with \uc2 American Express\uc2  off 84 cents, or 2%, to 40.38, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  down 27 cents, or 1.9%, to 13.68, and \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  off 40 cents, or 1%, to 40.72. The slip among financials came a day after the sector had rallied strongly.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite eked out a gain of 4.06, or 0.18%, to 2289.77. The Standard & Poor's 500 index slipped 0.80 point, or 0.07%, to 1121.10, with its financial sector leading the drop while technology and consumer companies rose.\par \par \uc2 The gains among consumer stocks came as a Commerce Department report showed U.S. retail sales rose for a second-straight month.\par \par \uc2 In addition, consumer-electronics retailer \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  posted a 61% jump in quarterly earnings, while supermarket operator \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's fiscal second-quarter profit surprisingly rose 2.8% on better-than-expected revenue, and \uc2 J.C. Penney\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mike Ullman\uc2  told attendees at a conference that the retailer expects better same-store sales in the fourth quarter than a year earlier. \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  rose 2.08, or 6%, to 36.73, while \uc2 Kroger\uc2  edged up 22 cents, or 1.1%, to 21.26, and \uc2 J.C. Penney\uc2  jumped 1.66, or 7.4%, to 23.99.\par \par \uc2 The better-than-expected reports from the retail sector represent "one of the signs people have been waiting for," said Frances Hudson, global thematic strategist at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2 . "You're seeing pockets of resilience."\par \par \uc2 Still, Hudson noted that investors remain concerned about the future of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "Quite a lot of the macro data is not supportive" of stocks, she said.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended flat Tuesday, as gains in electricity producers offset losses in property developers, after a report said the country's power industry is set for strong growth this year and recent data sparked concerns about further real-estate tightening measures from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade in a tight range between 2650 and 2750 this week, as investors will likely retreat to the sidelines on uncertainty about possible interest-rate hikes and further tightening measures aimed at the property market.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks ended marginally lower Tuesday as investors sold exporters on the surging yen while eagerly awaiting the results of the Democratic Party of Japan's presidential race after the market close.\par \par \uc2 Investors in Japanese stocks expressed general disappointment with Prime Minister Naoto Kan's victory in a party leadership race Tuesday, and said attention is now on Kan's abilities to improve the economy and stop the yen's crippling rise.\par \par \uc2 In a contest for presidency of the Democratic Party of Japan, Kan turned back a challenge from party veteran Ichiro Ozawa, winning 721 points versus Ozawa's 491. The race was open to party members only.\par \par \uc2 "This may be a negative for investor sentiment," said Shinichiro Matsushita, market analyst at Daiwa Securities.\par \par \uc2 During the evening session on the Osaka Securities Exchange, the Nikkei 225 December futures traded down 0.9% at 9170 as of at 0840 GMT, compared with the day-time closing price at 9250. The news came after the daily close of trading on the Tokyo Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Daiwa's Matsushita notes that the stock market will probably soon price in the result. Therefore, more importantly, Kan's leadership may be tested-- especially on whether he curbs the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 "People's first concern is the yen, and next the yen and the yen," said Susumu Kato, chief economist at Calyon Credit Agricole.\par \par \uc2 Kato said that if the Kan administration \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the yen weakens, and dollar rises to around Y90, the Nikkei Stock Average may trade between 9500 and 10,000.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures eased lower in U.S. trading Tuesday as uncertainty about the status of a major Canada-U.S. oil pipeline weighed on the market.\par \par \uc2 Investors propelled gold to record highs Tuesday as they continued buying the precious metal as a way to offset potential losses from a faltering global economic recovery and dollar.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange were broadly higher in late European trading, buoyed by a weaker dollar against the euro, and positive sentiment emanating from rising gold and equity markets, market participants said Tuesday. But volumes are relatively thin and trading remains rangebound across most of the metals, traders and analysts said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar slumped broadly Tuesday, sinking to a 15-year low against the yen, dipping below parity against the Swiss franc and falling to a one-month low against the euro as talk of possible Federal Reserve action to prop a slowing economy triggered a rush out of the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro jumped as much as 1.5 cents to above $1.29, breaking out of its recent range of $1.26 to $1.28.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar climbed to its highest level in two years against the greenback, while the U.K. pound gained nearly 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts pointed to a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (GS) conference call, during which Goldman said the Fed could announce a new asset-purchase program to kick-start a moribund U.S. economy, as contributing to the dollar's weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A new asset-purchase program, which Goldman said could start as early as November, would weigh further on the dollar because it would be taken as a signal that interest rates would remain ultralow for an extended period. Investors use cheap dollars to fund bets in higher-yielding assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same time, Fed action would solidify worries about a slowing U.S. economy, which could explain the bifurcated moves in markets Tuesday, with safe-harbor assets gaining alongside \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely tied to the pace of global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Slightly better than expected U.S. retail sales figures released Tuesday failed to allay worries over the pace of the U.S. recovery, said Alan Ruskin, global head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It does not detract from market concerns that another dose of [quantitative easing] still lies in the wings for later this year," Ruskin said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen gained after Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan survived a challenge to his leadership of the country's ruling party and retained his position as prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kan won roughly 60% of the available "points," beating Ichiro Ozawa, a power broker within the country's governing Democratic Party of Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to a fresh 15-year low against the yen, at Y82.92.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Provided the party closes ranks behind its leader, however, we believe the result represents a status quo scenario and do not expect any lasting impact on the markets from the election result itself," analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note to clients released late Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Now the focus shifts to party and Cabinet leadership appointments and how this might affect solidarity and policy positions in the ruling camp," they said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The likelihood that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken the yen has actually risen with Kan's victory, even though Ozawa was viewed as more eager to depress the yen via \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , market analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As he (Kan) is elected (and the uncertainty about his position has cleared), it is unlikely that he sits back and does nothing about the yen's strength. There is a higher possibility that he will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Shoji Hirakawa, chief strategist at UBS Securities, said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc was also in demand ahead of a meeting of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  later this week. Both the franc and the yen are serving as safe haven investments, at the expense of the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's decision to allow the yuan to rise recently may also have been taken as a signal to sell the greenback. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the dollar-yuan central parity, a daily reference rate, at 6.7378, a record low since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  began publishing the fixing in 1994 and down from the previous record low of 6.7509 Monday. In the Shanghai over-the-counter market, the dollar hit its lowest level against the yuan since trading started in 1994.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.3020 from $1.2873 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.08 from Y83.65, while the euro was at Y108.20 from Y107.66. The U.K. pound was at $1.5563 from $1.5429. The dollar was at CHF0.9950 from CHF1.0072.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traded at its lowest level in a month, at 81.074 from 81.870.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices rallied Tuesday despite better data as investors remained concerned about the long-term health of the economy and swept up low-risk debt. [ 14-09-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys climbed even as data showed that U.S. retail sales rose for a second-straight month in August, inventories at U.S. businesses surged during July and business sales posted the biggest gain in four months. Despite the better reports, investors were still left with the feeling that the economy could falter in the second half of the year, requiring the Federal Reserve to step in with more support.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, the two-year note was up 2/32 to yield 0.495%, the 10-year note was up 23/32 to yield 2.663%, and the 30-year was 1 5/32 higher to yield 3.783%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants Tuesday were focused on word from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., a primary dealer--one of the elite group of 18 banks that underwrite Treasury auctions and deal directly with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --that the Fed could announce a new program of asset purchases to support a weak economy as early as November. Jan Hatzius, chief economist at the bank, said Tuesday the Fed is likely to buy Treasurys worth around $1.0 trillion to kick-start the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury investors are still trying to figure out whether more support from the Fed will be necessary and have continued to scrutinize the economic data for clues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall, "the data are still not great," said Carl Lantz, a fixed-income strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, "and the threat of Fed purchases still looms. We still have accounts looking to buy any back-up" in price, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys started off Tuesday on a strong note after the dollar dropped to a fresh 15-year low against the yen following the victory of prime minister Naoto Kan, who is seen as less likely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem yen strength. Japanese investors were selling Treasurys ahead of the election to offset losses as Japanese government bonds weakened. The election helped JGBs though, and in turn, Treasury securities, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended steady Tuesday given the flat yield on one-year PBOC bills issued in the regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The bill sale result indicates the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has no plan to introduce new tightening measures, and it will likely keep the bill yields unchanged in the coming weeks," a Shanghai-based analyst with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's CNY28 billion, 10-year bond auction Wednesday might test investors' appetite for long-term debt, the analyst said, tipping a yield for the sale in a 3.30%-3.35% band compared with 3.33% in secondary market trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond yields fell in late trading on Tuesday after Prime Minister Naoto Kan, a champion of fiscal austerity, solidified his position as premier by winning re-election as head of the ruling Democratic Party of Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "JGB investors welcome Kan's reappointment as he has promised to restore the nation's fiscal health by raising taxes," Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts say investors are unlikely to actively buy back superlong-term debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even if Kan continues to be prime minister, he might have to take additional economic stimulus measures and could be forced to eventually issue more JGBs," said Masashi Shimominami, a bond market analyst at Mizuho Securities. "It's uncertain whether Kan's continuation in the premiership will help push superlong-term yields lower."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Business Inventories, Sales Rise In July \par US Retail Sales Rise In August, 2nd Straight Time \par NY Fed Group Offers Final Guides For Bond Trades \par Pentagon Changes Rules For Contracts \par Dimon Says Financial Reform Raises Customer Costs \par Judge Approves Madoff Trustee's $37.6M Fees \par WTO Likely To Rule \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  Got Illegal Subsidies \par Wall Street Blamed For Payday Loan Growth \par BofA CEO Says Changes Will Offset New Costs \par China Ming Yang Wind Power Details Estimated Terms For IPO \par =Country Style Cooking Files To Sell At Least 5M ADS In IPO \par =Market Players See Little To Cheer In Japan PM Kan Victory \par =Japan Stock Investors See Little Comfort In PM Kan's Victory \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2100  S Korea      Aug Department store sales \par 2245  New Zealand  Aug Electronic Card Transactions \par 2300  S Korea      Aug Economically Active Population Survey, incl \par                    Unemployment \par 0030  Australia    Sep \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Melbourne Institute Consumer Sentiment \par                    Survey \par 0100  Australia    Sep Skilled Vacancies Index \par 0130  Australia    2Q  Dwelling Unit Commencements \par 0130  Australia    Aug New Motor Vehicle Sales \par 0500  Singapore    Jul Retail Sales \par 0800  Hong Kong    Aug Hong Kong port container throughput monthly \par                    estimates \par 0830  UK           Aug Monthly UK unemployment figures \par 0900  EU           Aug Harmonised CPI \par 0900  EU           2Q  Employment \par 1100  US           Sep 10 MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1200  US           Former Fed Chmn Greenspan speaks at Council on Foreign \par                    Relations in New York \par 1230  US           Sep Empire State Manufacturing Survey \par 1230  US           Aug Import & Export Price Indexes \par 1315  US           Aug Industrial Production & Capacity Utilization \par 1430  US           Sep 10 EIA Weekly Petroleum Status Report \par N/A