\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN DPJ ELECTION RESULT NOT CREDIT POSITIVE - S&P \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Naoto Kan's victory Tuesday in a ruling party leadership race, which will keep him in the prime minister's office for a while longer--is not a positive signal "at all" for Japan's credit rating, given the party's big-spending promises, said a senior sovereign analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services. (published 0752 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER:1Q-3Q DATA CAN HELP DECIDE ON KEEPING STIMULUS POLICY-XINHUA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should wait to see the economic data for the first three quarters of the year before deciding on whether to continue with its fiscal stimulus policy, Jia Kang, the head of the Institute of Fiscal Science at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was cited as saying by the state-run Xinhua News Agency on Tuesday. (published 0740 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREATENS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS YEN HITS NEW 15-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threatened again Tuesday to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market after the yen pushed to a fresh 15-year high against the dollar, keeping traders on their toes ahead of a ruling party leadership election later in the day that could trigger more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility. (published 0316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KAN BEATS BACK OZAWA CHALLENGE TO REMAIN JAPAN PM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan beat back a challenge to his hold on power Tuesday, handily defeating longtime powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa for leadership of the ruling party. (published 0833 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE JULY ANNUAL INDUS OUTPUT RISE EASES FROM JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial production in the 16 countries that use the euro rose at a slower year-on-year pace in July than in June, as all but one sector posted a slower gain despite a continued strong performance in Germany and France, data showed Tuesday. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE WAGE GROWTH SLOWED IN 2Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wage growth in the 16 countries that use the euro slowed in the three months to the end of June, contributing to a deceleration in the rate of increase of labor costs for euro-zone businesses. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GERMAN ZEW SEP INDICATOR FALLS SHARPLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expectations for Germany's economy fell to their lowest level in a year and a half in September, as experts reacted dramatically to signs of a slowdown in the country's core manufacturing sector, the ZEW research institute said Tuesday. (published 0941 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S SCHAEUBLE:WON'T SEE THIS YEAR'S HIGH GROWTH RATE IN 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economy is well positioned for consistent growth, but the pace of expansion in 2011 won't match the extremely high rate that is likely this year, Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said Tuesday. (published 0830 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SOARING SWISS FRANC HITS PARITY AGAINST DOLLAR - EBS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sky-high Swiss franc hit a major new milestone Tuesday, shooting up to parity against the dollar for the first time since a spell of broad-based dollar weakness in December 2009. (published 0854 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE WEALE: UK ECONOMIC RECOVERY LIKELY TO BE FITFUL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee member Martin Weale said Tuesday that the U.K. economic recovery will be "fitful" and that unemployment could rise more than official forecasts indicate. (published 1048 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK AUGUST INFLATION HIGHER THAN EXPECTED AT 3.1% YY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer price inflation remained at least a percentage point above the Bank of England's target for the eighth consecutive month in August, boosted by airfares, and clothing and food prices. (published 1001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH MINISTER SEES 2010 DEFICIT, GDP BETTER THAN FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Francois Baroin Tuesday said France will better its forecasts for gross domestic product and the public deficit this year. (published 0623 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: FRENCH AUGUST CPI +0.2% ON MONTH; +1.4% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French consumer price index rose less than expected in August, official data showed Tuesday. (published 0535 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRISH GOVERNMENT INDICATES 2011 BUDGET COULD TOP EUR3B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Irish Prime Minister Brian Cowen said Tuesday that Ireland's 2011 budget spending cuts, due to be announced Dec. 7, could top the targeted EUR3 billion, but won't be as much as EUR4 billion. (published 0937 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB FUNDING FOR SPAIN BANKS EUR109.8B AUGUST VS EUR130.2B JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish banks' recourse to European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funding fell in August as international capital markets started opening up again for Spanish institutions. (published 0843 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA YUAN HITS FRESH MODERN-ERA HIGH VS DOLLAR TUESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan hit another high against the U.S. dollar Tuesday after China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate lower for the third straight trading session amid the dollar's global weakness and growing U.S. pressure over Beijng's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA AUGUST INFLATION EASES; RBI TIGHTENING EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A revamped wholesale price index, launched Tuesday, showed Indian inflation eased in August but remained uncomfortably high, raising the odds that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will hike rates this week to contain spreading demand pressures. (published 0757 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA MINISTER HAS AGREED TO CONSIDER EXPLORATION IN MINING TAX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Energy Minister Martin Ferguson said Tuesday his center-left Labor government has agreed to consider revisiting some aspects of a proposed tax on mining company profits, such as an exploration rebate. (published 0641 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: BUSINESS GROUPS TO FIGHT US JOB PLANS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Multinational companies and small-business interests are working in concert to oppose some of the Obama administration's jobs proposals. (published 0008 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : YEN RISES ON KAN'S REELECTION; LITTLE MOMENTUM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Naoto Kan's reelection as leader of Japan's ruling DPJ, keeping him as prime minister, pushed the yen up to a new 15-year high against the dollar. (published 0933 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: THE U.S. ISN'T GOOD FOR RISK APPETITE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk takers beware! There may be a lot to celebrate at the moment. But there is also still a lot to worry about. Yes, there is greater market optimism about both the strength of the global recovery as well as the health of the European banking system. News from China, the European Commission and banking regulators over the last few days have all helped to do that. But there is still one big problem--the U.S. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERVIEW: INDONESIA AIMS FOR 66% RISE IN TRADE WITH CHINA IN 3-5 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia aims to increase its annual trade with China by more than 66% to $50 billion in three to five years from around $30 billion now after a free-trade agreement between the countries took full effect in January, Indonesia Trade Minister Mari Pangestu said Tuesday. (published 0654 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO EXTEND RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Monday's recovery off 129.53 leaves a bull hammer on the daily chart, and threatens to extend the rally towards resistance at 130.50. The 38.2% retracement level of the 132.14/129.53 decline lies in the 130.50 area, and only a break above 130.50 would lift the tone, opening 131.00. The 130.01 level has become near-term pivotal support, and a break below 129.77 is needed to bring the focus back onto the 129.53 low. Weekly chart BUND trend: Bearish. (published 0649 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: MORE 15-YEAR LOWS FOR USD/JPY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The Sep. 6 reaction high at 1.2920 is the focus following Monday's strong gains. Resistance at 1.2893 is under threat as EUR bulls look to build on the recovery off last Friday's low at 1.2642 and re-open the 1.2920 peak. 1.2969/75 would become the target area on a break above 1.2920. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 1.2793 and only below there would concern near-term bulls. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Range. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHARTING ASIA:EURO MAY RISE TO NZ$1.7923,NZ$1.8242 NEAR-TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose to a four-session high against the Kiwi dollar Tuesday after some disappointing New Zealand retail sales data. Technical charts suggest the euro, quoted at NZ$1.7666 at 0606 GMT Tuesday, could advance to the 55-day moving average on the euro/Kiwi dollar daily chart, now at NZ$1.7923, and possibly to NZ$1.8242 in coming days. (published 0631 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  COULD PROMPT COPYCATS AROUND ASIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets might well fail to weaken the yen, but should have a near-term impact on other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FOCUS: AUSTRALIAN HOUSING AFFORDABILITY CREATES POLICY HEADACHE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Big Four banks are likely to raise their mortgage rates beyond the amount of any new rate hikes from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , presenting the new Labor government with one of its first major policy challenges. (published 0255 GMT) [ 14-09-10 1104GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100914e69e000tu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
