\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,233 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 09:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREATENS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS YEN HITS NEW 15-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  threatened again Tuesday to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market after the yen pushed to a fresh 15-year high against the dollar, keeping traders on their toes ahead of a ruling party leadership election later in the day that could trigger more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility. (published 0316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KAN DECLARED WINNER OF DPJ VOTE, TO REMAIN JAPAN PRIME MINISTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Naoto Kan won a fiercely contested party leadership race Tuesday, defeating political heavyweight Ichiro Ozawa to secure his reelection as Japan's prime minister. (published 0643 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PM CHALLENGER OZAWA CALLS FOR BOJ LAW CHANGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese prime-minister hopeful Ichiro Ozawa signalled he would push for legal changes to force the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to do more to end years of deflation in Japan if he wins a ruling party leadership race Tuesday. (published 0615 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH MINISTER SEES 2010 DEFICIT, GDP BETTER THAN FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Francois Baroin Tuesday said France will better its forecasts for gross domestic product and the public deficit this year. (published 0623 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK AUGUST HOUSE PRICE BALANCE SLUMPS TO -32 VS JULY -8 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. house prices fell sharply in August by the largest single month drop since between April and May 1989, and to the lowest level in over a year as another increase in the number of properties for sale and a steeper drop in buyer demand pushed prices lower, the Royal Institution of Chartered Surveyors said Tuesday. (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: FRENCH AUGUST CPI +0.2% ON MONTH; +1.4% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The French consumer price index rose less than expected in August, official data showed Tuesday. (published 0535 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA YUAN HITS FRESH MODERN-ERA HIGH VS DOLLAR TUESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan hit another high against the U.S. dollar Tuesday after China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate lower for the third straight trading session amid the dollar's global weakness and growing U.S. pressure over Beijng's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA MINISTER HAS AGREED TO CONSIDER EXPLORATION IN MINING TAX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Energy Minister Martin Ferguson said Tuesday his center-left Labor government has agreed to consider revisiting some aspects of a proposed tax on mining company profits, such as an exploration rebate. (published 0641 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA AUGUST BUSINESS CONFIDENCE INDEX UP 9 POINTS TO +11 -NAB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business confidence rose strongly in August, according to a survey released Tuesday, despite a period of political uncertainty as the country was left wondering what the make-up of the next government would be. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NZ RETAIL SALES NO IMPETUS FOR RBNZ TO HIKE RATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand retail sales fell in July, failing to meet expectations and giving little impetus for the Reserve Bank of New Zealand to start hiking the official cash rate before December. (published 0224 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: SEPT ON-YEAR INFLATION LIKELY BELOW 6% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's on-year inflation in September will likely ease below 6%, from 6.44% in August, but the full-year target of 6% seems "hard to achieve," Bank Indonesia deputy governor Hartadi Sarwono said Tuesday. (published 0341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NZ AUGUST MEDIAN HOUSE PRICE NZ$350,000 VS NZ$349,000 JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subdued home sales in August compared to July and relatively flat home prices, coupled with tepid retail sales data Tuesday, further underscore that the Reserve Bank of New Zealand will likely keep key rates on hold at 3.0% at Thursday's monetary-policy review. (published 2346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK CONSUMER CONFIDENCE STRENGTHENED IN AUGUST - NATIONWIDE BUILDING SOCIETY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumers became less pessimistic about the outlook for the economy, the jobs market and their future incomes during August, according to a monthly survey conducted by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nationwide Building Society\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: BUSINESS GROUPS TO FIGHT US JOB PLANS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Multinational companies and small-business interests are working in concert to oppose some of the Obama administration's jobs proposals. (published 0008 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINES TO ISSUE TREASURY BOND DUE SEPT 2020 AT 6.125% YIELD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines decided to reject some bids to lower yield on a new 10-year treasury bond auctioned Tuesday, just days after the government raised $1 billion from its maiden peso-denominated global bond. (published 0544 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par INTERVIEW: INDONESIA AIMS FOR 66% RISE IN TRADE WITH CHINA IN 3-5 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia aims to increase its annual trade with China by more than 66% to $50 billion in three to five years from around $30 billion now after a free-trade agreement between the countries took full effect in January, Indonesia Trade Minister Mari Pangestu said Tuesday. (published 0654 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO EXTEND RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Monday's recovery off 129.53 leaves a bull hammer on the daily chart, and threatens to extend the rally towards resistance at 130.50. The 38.2% retracement level of the 132.14/129.53 decline lies in the 130.50 area, and only a break above 130.50 would lift the tone, opening 131.00. The 130.01 level has become near-term pivotal support, and a break below 129.77 is needed to bring the focus back onto the 129.53 low. Weekly chart BUND trend: Bearish. (published 0649 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: MORE 15-YEAR LOWS FOR USD/JPY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The Sep. 6 reaction high at 1.2920 is the focus following Monday's strong gains. Resistance at 1.2893 is under threat as EUR bulls look to build on the recovery off last Friday's low at 1.2642 and re-open the 1.2920 peak. 1.2969/75 would become the target area on a break above 1.2920. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 1.2793 and only below there would concern near-term bulls. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Range. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHARTING ASIA:EURO MAY RISE TO NZ$1.7923,NZ$1.8242 NEAR-TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose to a four-session high against the Kiwi dollar Tuesday after some disappointing New Zealand retail sales data. Technical charts suggest the euro, quoted at NZ$1.7666 at 0606 GMT Tuesday, could advance to the 55-day moving average on the euro/Kiwi dollar daily chart, now at NZ$1.7923, and possibly to NZ$1.8242 in coming days. (published 0631 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  COULD PROMPT COPYCATS AROUND ASIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets might well fail to weaken the yen, but should have a near-term impact on other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FOCUS: AUSTRALIAN HOUSING AFFORDABILITY CREATES POLICY HEADACHE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Big Four banks are likely to raise their mortgage rates beyond the amount of any new rate hikes from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , presenting the new Labor government with one of its first major policy challenges. (published 0255 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: RBNZ TIPPED TO STAND PAT, HIGHLIGHT UNCERTAIN OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists widely expect the Reserve Bank of New Zealand to keep its Official Cash Rate unchanged at 3.0% Thursday, while highlighting the uncertain economic outlook in its accompanying statement, particularly after the 7.0-magnitude earthquake in the country's second-largest city of Christchurch earlier this month. (published 0415 GMT) [ 14-09-10 0704GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100914e69e000gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
