 Yasukawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders say market will become more cautious about possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  if pair falls below 83.00, which could help support pair. Add, they will pay close attention to Japan's moves to curb JPY strength under PM Kan's new administration, which now being formed. Says pair may trade in 82.70-84.00 range vs 83.07 last. Due to broader USD weakness, EUR/USD biased up; may trade in 1.2950-1.3050 vs 1.2993, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Says EUR/JPY may trade in 107.00-109.00 vs 107.85. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to downside after settling down 39 cents yesterday at $76.80/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode, but stochastics turning bearish at overbought level. Support at $76.08 (100-day moving average), then at $75.96 (previous cap set Thursday); breach would expose downside to $73.88 (Thursday's low), then $73.37 (Sept. 8 low), $72.63 (Sept. 7 low) and $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low). Resistance at $76.97 (hourly chart), then at $78.04 (1-month high set Monday); breach would reinstate near-term positive outlook, exposing upside to $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline), then psychological $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). October crude last down 51 cents at $76.29/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ August Electronic Card Transaction data, alongside July retail sales released yesterday, point to gradual recovery in retail spending, says ASB economist Christina Leung; "the continued gradual pace of the recovery in retail spending reinforces our expectations the RBNZ will leave the official cash rate on hold until the start of next year." Stats NZ says card transactions excluding car related purchases fell 0.2%. Leung says decline show households yet to bring forward purchases of major household items in anticipation of GST increase to 15% vs 12.5% in October; "either people are leaving a pre-GST spend-up until the last minute or the timing effect on retail spending will be muted." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper continues to be most favoured base metal, says Citi, and "we find it hard to fault its underlying fundamentals"; says Chinese import data with refined copper consumption growing at more than 20% is supportive, OECD demand picking up while supply remains constrained and inventories continue to be drawn down with LME warehouse stocks of copper now at 390,000 metric tons, equivalent to one month of Chinese demand. All up, this remains supportive for copper, with red metal expected to trade $6,000-$8,000 ton range rest of 2010, "with potential upside risk." LME 3-month copper rose $20 vs Monday PM kerb to $7,650 while aluminum added $30 at $2,160, zinc gained $31 to $2,173, nickel surged $500 to $23,350, tin climbed $350 to $22,645; lead eased $9 to $2,242. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may drop to 82.70 as U.S. Treasury yields fall overnight (2-year at 0.495%), narrowing U.S.-Japan yield spread, making USD less attractive, says Takeshi Yasukawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders say market will become more cautious about possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  if pair falls below 83.00, which could help support pair. Add, they will pay close attention to Japan's moves to curb JPY strength under PM Kan's new administration, which now being formed. Says pair may trade in 82.70-84.00 range vs 83.07 last. Due to broader USD weakness, EUR/USD biased down; may trade in 1.2950-1.3050 vs 1.2993, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Says EUR/JPY may trade in 107.00-109.00 vs 107.85. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs may rise as fiscally-conservative PM Kan's victory vs Ozawa, who's considered fiscal expansionist, in ruling party's leadership race Tuesday means eased concerns about risk of massive government spending, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief bond strategist Jun Ishii. Adds, slightly weak U.S. shares also positive for JGBs. Tips December futures to trade in 141.65-141.90 range vs 141.38 last. 10-year yield may trade in 1.070%-1.100% range vs 1.105% last.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD slumped broadly Tuesday, sinking to 15-year low vs JPY, dipping below parity vs CHF, falling to 1-month low vs EUR as talk of possible Fed action to prop slowing economy triggered rush out of USD. Some analysts pointed to \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  comments in conference call that Fed could announce new asset-purchase program to kick-start economy. JPY benefited from reelection Japan's PM Kan in ruling party elections, he's considered less likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem JPY strength. EUR/USD 1.3020 vs 1.2873 late Monday, USD/JPY at 83.08 vs 83.65, EUR/JPY at 108.20 vs 107.66, GBP/USD at 1.5563 vs 1.5429, USD/CHF at 0.9950 vs 1.0072. USD Index at 81.074 from 81.870. Most stocks fell slightly on worried about economy despite positive signs from retail sector. \uc2 American Express\uc2  off 2%, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  down 1.9%, \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  off 1%. Dow slips 0.2%, Nasdaq +0.2%, Philly semicons +1.7%. Treasury prices rallied as investors remained concerned about economy, swept up low-risk debt; 2-year yields down 4.0 bps at 0.495%, 10-years down 8.3 bps at 2.663%. Investors propelled gold to record highs, buying to offset potential losses from faltering global economic recovery and USD; December Comex gold rose $24.60 to $1,271.70/oz. Crude-oil futures eased as uncertainty about status of a major Canada-U.S. oil pipeline weighed; October Nymex crude settled 39 cents lower at $76.80/bbl. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 5.6 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 4th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 25.4 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 19.8 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall after USD/JPY dipped below 83 overnight (now 83.08) following PM Kan's win in ruling Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election Tuesday. "The key issue now is whether Kan can move speedily to implement policies" on curbing yen's rise and lowering corporate taxes, says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. "There is no time to lose." Puts Nikkei range for today at 9100-9300 vs 9299.31 close yesterday (down 0.2%). Market attention now turning to new Kan Cabinet lineup, which may spur political realignment if members close to former challenger Ichiro Ozawa are excluded. Analysts say Nikkei support level pegged at 9000, helped by recent batch of better-than-expected U.S. data, including August retail sales. Gold-linked shares such as \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  (5713.TO) likely to rise after gold hit fresh record high overnight. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9295 vs Osaka close at 9250. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads slightly bearish, with overnight SPI 200 futures down 0.2% at 4630 after Wall Street fell despite better-than-expected U.S. economic data. DJIA down 0.2% at 10526.49 (first fall in 5 days), S&P 500 down 0.1% at 1121.1, Nasdaq up 0.2% at 2289.77. Germany's DAX 30 rose 0.2% but ZEW economic expectations index fell to -4.3 in September (lowest in around 18 months), vs 9.0 expected. U.S. 10-year bond yield fell 8 points to 2.66% as investors focussed on \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' forecast of further U.S. quantitative easing as soon as November. August U.S. retail sales rose 0.4% vs 0.3% expected. July business inventories in U.S. rose 1.0% vs 0.8% expected. S&P 500 Financials down 1.0%, with KBW Bank index down 1.7%. 500 Materials down 0.1%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 0.3%, Vale down 0.7%, Freeport down 0.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 3.0%, Barrick up 2.3%. BHP ADRs at A$39.14 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9395) vs local close at A$39.05. London listed BHP up 0.6%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 0.1%. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.3%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.3%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 0.1%, with \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  up 6% on strong quarterly earnings and \uc2 J.C. Penney\uc2  up 7.4% after forecasting better same-store sales in 4Q. Spot gold at [ 09-14-10 1944ET ]\par \par \uc2 US$1,268.53 vs US$1,245.73. Nymex crude oil down 0.5% at US$76.80. LME copper up 0.3%, zinc up 1.5%, aluminum up 1.4%, nickel up 2.1%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.2993 vs 1.2877. AUD/USD at 0.9398 vs 0.9348. S&P/ASX 200 last 4626.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 1944ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100914e69e000ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
