\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Target 82.85 Post-Kan Vote - BBH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 08:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] News PM Kan has won DPJ election in Japan has USD/JPY down around 83.40 and targeting 82.85 support, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Kan was seen as having won the wider member ballot, but the election among Japanese parliament members was considered to be extremely close before the event. What does this mean for the yen? Well, Kan appears to be less strident about the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which will give yen bulls some encouragement"; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be a risky road for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as would almost certainly be without the support of other major central banks, thus with limited chance of success. (Rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY just printed a fresh 15-year low of 83.09 on news that PM Kan has won the ruling party leadership vote. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts Axel Rudolph says the spot now has risk toward its 2008-2010 support line that comes in at 82.82 and this is the last defence ahead of the 79.92 1995 low. Topside Rudolph pegs resistance at Monday's 84.45 highs.(gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks paring losses as players nibbling on dips. Market breadth flat vs slightly negative at end of morning session. STI off 0.3% at 3058.13, expected to hold above 3043 August high. "Trades are likely to stay in the red for the rest of the afternoon on the back of negative Dow futures. However, excessive downside is likely to be capped by bargain-hunting activities," says CIMB. Among blue chips, SGX (S68.SG) best performer, +3.7% at S$8.22, on prospect of increased trading activity as bourse operator enables ADRs of 19 major Asian companies to be quoted in Singapore from Oct. 22. Most active stocks in market still Genting group, small caps, including \uc2 Kreuz Holdings\uc2  (5RK.SG), +8.6% at S$0.44, Vallianz (545.SG), +9.1% at S$0.18, \uc2 Spice i2i\uc2  (M09.SG), +4.0% at S$0.13. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] Kan wins fiercely contested ruling DPJ party leadership race, defeating political heavyweight Ozawa to secure reelection as Japan PM; result brings some political stability in a country that has had 5 leaders in 4 years--and 2 since DPJ took power for first time a year ago. Kan wins 721 points of 1,222 points up for grabs in DPJ presidential race. All eyes on how backroom dealer Ozawa may react, with some analysts saying can't completely rule out he may bolt the DPJ, triggering political upheaval, realignment in Japan politics; government faces immense policy challenges, from growing mountain of public debt to entrenched deflation, surge in JPY to 15-year highs vs USD that threatens export-dependent economy. Kan win suggests no immediate shift in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy; expect strident MOF warnings vs JPY rise but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if yen abruptly lurches higher in move authorities determine speculative. (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] The December bund contract is 0.41 higher at 130.33, but Richard Adcock at \uc2 UBS\uc2  says with the confirmation of the weekly engulfing pattern, upside will be limited in time and extent before the downside is resumed. The 50%/62% retracements of Friday's bearish range stand at 130.43 and 130.54, and we would suggest this as the first potential failure area from which a resumption the downside could be seen, Adcock says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD tad higher to 7.7677 vs 7.7672 yesterday as local bank buys on corporate demand, says trader at local bank. Tips pair in 7.7650-7.7690 band near term. "Still, the pair will likely stay in a tight range with trade quiet and biased down tracking the lower USD/CNY fixing. I've spotted a U.S. bank selling dollars at 7.7680 this morning, dragging the pair to 7.7670 but the sale wasn't sizable," trader says. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] The Netherlands has issued EUR43 billion of government bonds, or DSLs, so far in 2010, equating to 86% of the expected total issuance of EUR50 billion, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , ahead of the EUR2.5 billion to EUR3.5 billion auction of the DSL 1.75% January 2013 bond Tuesday. This completion level is ahead of the euro zone average of 79%, it says. \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  adds Dutch paper has been outperforming both French and Finnish paper in the third quarter, with the 10-year spread to bund reaching a low of 16 basis points. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] The appetite for Greek assets will be tested Tuesday as the Greek Public Debt Management Agency holds its first monthly T-bill auction. Pricing of the auction is tricky due to the lack of liquidity in the secondary market for Greek government paper, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  bank, and "it is very difficult to guess a rate for the 26-week GTB supply on offer today." Last week, the Irish T-bill auction led to a weakening in peripheral bonds all the way down the curve. However, risk/reward favors buy-and-hold investors, says David Schnautz, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist. "This is also in line with our strategic recommendation to position for peripheral curve steepening going forward." The 10-year Greek/German bond yield spread is 2.5 bps up on the day at 896 bps, Irish/German is 3 bps wider at 355.5 bps, Portuguese/German is unchanged at 329 bps, Spanish/German is 2 bps up at 174 bps and Italian/German is up 2 bps at 147 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI little changed vs midday, +0.2% at 20,707.86, rising for 9th session out of 10, though profit-taking pressure keeps index from testing early August high of 21,800. \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2  expects HK shares to consolidate recent gains first; also, "some funds may switch to IPO activities which are heating up." Market volume tapers off vs yesterday at HK$40.20 billion. \uc2 Foxconn International\uc2  (2038.HK) plays catch-up, +7.0% at HK$5.64 -- best-performing blue chip; Cnooc (0883.HK) succumbing to profit-taking, down 0.5% at HK$14.62 after 9.1% rally in past 3 sessions. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are higher Tuesday with stocks set to open down. The economic calendar also picks up after scant data in past few sessions, with the ZEW survey and euro-zone industrial production data due at 0900 GMT. Recent improvement in risk sentiment has been underpinned by better than expected data in the US, and the market will be closely eyeing retail sales data at 1230 GMT for clues, ahead of industrial production and regional surveys later in the week. In the primary market, Greece will offer EUR900M of T-bills, having seen its yield premium over bunds tighten noticeably on Monday. The Netherlands also offers up to EUR3.5B of 3-year bonds. December bunds are up 0.39 at 130.31 and the 10-year bund is up at 98.80 to yield 2.383%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] Slowdown in India inflation not as big as expected despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising (repo) rate by 100 basis points since January, says Brian Jackson, senior emerging markets strategist with RBC. Strong industrial output data last week, high August inflation suggest RBI likely to hike rates again at mid-quarter review Thursday, he says. "We forecast a hike of 25 basis points this week, followed by another 25 basis point hike later in the year," says Jackson. India's wholesale price inflation decelerated to 9.5% in August vs 9.97% in July, just below median 9.6% forecast in Dow Jones poll (as per old series). (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] RBI may not hike rates at approaching monetary policy review, as previous rate hikes help temper the risks of the economy overheating, while easing global commodity prices may also help keep prices in check, says D.K. Joshi, principal economist at Crisil. "A hike at the mid-quarter review indicates rapid tightening, and I don't see any reason for the RBI to risk growth with such a move," says Joshi. Adds, new inflation index may help keep headline numbers in check as higher weight to manufacturing helps moderate impact of volatile food prices on monthly data prints.(bijou.george@dowjones.com) [ 09-14-10 0254ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100914e69e00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
