\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:China Individual Ppty Stks May Diverge Further -CS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 03:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  says China's primary housing transaction volumes continued to rebound in most cities last week (September 6-12), but magnitude of rebound was "much less" than previous weeks with Beijing +9% on week, Shanghai +1%, Shenzhen +26%, Guangzhou +3%. Also notes, in secondary market, more home buyers returned to a wait-and-see stance; "as most cities' secondary housing prices increased, secondary housing transaction volumes there dropped significantly on-week." House continues to believe that individual stock's performance may diverge further, prefers those with high asset turns, healthy gearing such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Vanke\f28 \uc2 \f2  (000002.SZ), KWG (1813.HK). Vanke last down 0.8% at CNY8.28 in Shenzhen, while KWG flat at HK$6.20 pre-market in HK. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares moving higher, helped by overnight gains on Wall Street; benchmark PSEi +0.7% to 4000.56 in moderate volume, with gainers swamping losers, 58 to 17; driving market advance are property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH), up 2.2% to PHP2.38, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 3.6% to PHP5.75, and \uc2 Globe Telecom\uc2  (GLO.PH), up 0.6% to PHP875. "Positive sentiment from global markets last night is helping our market. We've tracked historical up moves of the market, and they showed 50%-60% rise from the bottom. Figures suggest up to 4200 for the PSEi this time," says Ron Rodrigo, research head at DBP-Daiwa Securities; but also warns of correction along the way with market already at highs. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures down 0.2% at 21,687, likely taken by most as normal consolidation, as HSI up in 8 out of past 9 sessions (cumulative gains 5.5%). Contract ends yesterday at 62-point premium to cash, continuing to flag near-term bullishness. September fair value at less than single-digit discount to cash with Swire Pacific (0019.HK) going ex-dividend today; open interest increases 2,730 lots to 103,286. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY hits fresh modern-era low of 6.7470 in early trading, after PBOC sets central parity at yet another record low amid renewed global USD weakness, growing U.S. pressure over CNY policy. Now at 6.7505 vs 6.7618 yesterday close; central parity at 6.7378 vs 6.7509, down for 4th consecutive session. "The central parity's continued slide does send people a signal, but we remain clueless how long the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants the downtrend to be," says Guangzhou-based trader at foreign bank. Notes wave of bargain hunting for USD shortly after open, but skeptical such buying suggests PBOC support behind. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian business confidence rose strongly in August despite period of political uncertainty following inconclusive elections Aug. 21. But business conditions stayed patchy with employment and retail sales weak, amplifying concerns about two-speed economy. NAB survey showed business confidence rose 9 points from July to +11 points, while business conditions remained unchanged at +5 points. Capacity utilization fell to 81.2% in August from 82.3% in July. Survey was conducted between Aug. 24 and August 31, covering 530 firms in the non-farm sector. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares nearly flat early, as losses by real estate developers amid continued concerns about possibility of further tightening measures offset gains by electricity producers on earnings optimism. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.02% at 2687.76, tipped to stay in 2650-2750 range for now. "The market is still worried about another round of property tightening, if prices don't stay down in the next month," says Zhang Suyu at Dongxing Securities. Electricity companies slightly higher after Shanghai Securities News reports industry set to grow 20% this year. Among actives, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 1.2% at CNY8.25, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) down 1.4% at CNY11.26. \uc2 China XD Electric\uc2  (601179.SH) +1.3% at CNY7.30. Shenzhen index flat at 1206.22. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower at 8,955 vs 9,015 on Wednesday last week before market shut for long holidays, with dealers citing USD's general weakness; say pair seems to have found support around current levels as Bank Indonesia has been spotted bidding for USD at IDR8,950, though no takers as yet. "It seems Bank Indonesia doesn't want the dollar to fall too quickly," dealer says. Pair tipped in 8,950-8,970 band. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] Beijing likely to roll out new tightening measures to stunt housing price growth in major cities as sales, prices rebound over next few months during real-estate peak season, says Nicholas Consonery at Eurasia. Says, harder line against developers possible, but new moves likely cautious, locally-oriented. "But any new moves will most likely be cautious and locally-oriented. Beijing will want to avoid further derailing the broader nationwide housing market, since aggressive tightening from April has already been largely effective in stabilizing prices and slowing transactions and sales," Consonery says. Adds, "the offsetting variable is the outside chance of a deposit interest rate hike, following this weekend's uptick in consumer price inflation." (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall below 83.00 soon as investors actively buying JPY amid growing expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market if PM Kan stays in his post, says Masanobu Ishikawa, senior dealer at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow; "As speculation grows that Kan will remain prime minister, many investors appear to be buying the yen." Says buying broad-based among foreign, domestic financial institutions. Unless Kan shows strong commitment to curbing current appreciation of yen, investors "won't stop buying" JPY, he says. DPJ presidential race result likely around 0630-0700 GMT. Citibank Japan chief strategy Osamu Takashima also eyeing 83.00 for USD/JPY if Kan wins; adds, halt in recent gains in Europe, U.S. bond yields making USD, EUR less attractive; says Japan exporters also behind USD sales. Meanwhile, EUR/JPY falling as investors take profits after recent surge caused by upward revision of euro-zone GDP growth forecast yesterday, strong China economic data at weekend, says Naoto Taikoji, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . EUR/USD last 107.22. USD/JPY weaker at 83.36, though off 15-year low of 83.25 marked earlier in day. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD rangebound as European bank and U.S. bank selling USD, though major U.K. bank also buying, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in tight 7.7640-7.7690 range near term. "Still, USD/HKD remains downward biased tracking lower USD/CNY, as the freely convertible Hong Kong dollar is often traded as a proxy for the yuan," says trader. Pair last at 7.7675 vs 7.7672 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks succumb to light profit-taking after yesterday's broad-based gains, which pushed STI to new 2010 high. Benchmark off 0.1% at 3062.67, with support tipped at previous year-to-date high of 3043 set in August. Any rebound may be capped at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "Until we get macro news that is consistent, we believe equities markets will be choppy," says DBS Asia strategist Joanne Goh; but notes unless global economic conditions deteriorate to an outright recession, which will force investment portfolios to reduce allocation of riskier assets, "we do not see much downside from current levels." Genting group remains most active in market, with Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG), off 2.9% at US$495, Genting Singapore (G13.SG) flat at S$1.99 after yesterday's rally to multi-year highs. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude steady in electronic trading. Traders on sidelines, awaiting U.S. economic indicators due later today including retail sales, says Daiichi Shohin analyst Koichi Murakami; adds after data from China showed healthy growth, market now watching how U.S. does. "Though China is important, the U.S. is the main driver because emerging countries cannot grow without exporting to the U.S." October Nymex crude last down 1 cent at $77.18/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com) [ 09-13-10 2158ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100914e69e0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
