ai (2317.TW) +0.8% at NT$116.00, TSMC (2330.TW) +0.2% at NT$61.00, \uc2 Mega Financial\uc2  (2886.TW) +1.0% at NT$20.70, Chinatrust (2891.TW) +0.3% at NT$19.45. "But should turnover spike and gains fail to materialize, profit-taking will likely emerge." (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, likely taking cue from overnight gains on Wall Street; benchmark PSEi may head towards 4000 resistance after closing at new high 3972.60, up 1.8% to extend current run-up to nearly 12%, 9th days of gains in row; immediate support 3950. Banks, utilities and property stocks such as Megaworld (MEG.PH), \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH) and Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH) may continue to lead advance; Grace Cerdena, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia.com, warns investors to closely watch the market's momentum as it approaches 4000 level as some could start pocketing gains. "We may have moved too fast, too soon," says Cerdena. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY finds some support from bids appearing after earlier lurch downward, but pair remains heavy due to general USD weakness, short-term players may target sell-stops, opening up further downside, say sales trader at Japanese bank; adds, shouldn't rule out dip below Y83, but still relatively mild move unlikely to prompt MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he says; Eyes on 20-year JGB auction at 0345 GMT, which some market participants say could be a risk if view takes hold that big-spender Ozawa has chance to beat PM Kan in today's DPJ leadership race; weak auction result would push up bond yields, invite JPY buying, he says. For now though, trader says market generally pricing in a Kan victory. (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.4% at 4634.7, fading after early rise to 4-month high of 4649.1 as offshore markets turn negative. In Asia, Nikkei is down about 0.4%, while U.S. stock index futures have turned slightly negative after early gains. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, AUD/USD is down 0.4% and EUR/USD down 0.2% on NY closing levels. 200-day moving average near 4629 offers tentative support. Traders looking to see if S&P/ASX 200 turns negative this afternoon, ahead of U.S. retail sales data. S&P/ASX 200 has risen 10 of the last 14 days, gaining as much as 7.% in that period. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rebounds to 1,162.7 from an early low of 1,159.0 vs 1,160.8 late yesterday in Seoul, after initial attempt at breaching 1,160 support fails due to players becoming increasingly wary over possible USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities at levels below 1,160. Anticipation of possible USD-buying related to recent M&A deals also supporting pair, traders say. Local bank trader expects pair to seesaw in 1,158-1,164 range rest of session; "I don't see much exporters' dollar-selling yet," though they may kick in later in afternoon, he says. Kospi now flat, unable to provide momentum for KRW so far today. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 09-13-10 2149ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100914e69e0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
