\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Falls, 83.00 Level Eyed - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 03:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall below 83.00 soon as investors actively buying JPY amid growing expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in market if PM Kan stays in his post, says Masanobu Ishikawa, senior dealer at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow; "As speculation grows that Kan will remain prime minister, many investors appear to be buying the yen." Says buying broad-based among foreign, domestic financial institutions. Unless Kan shows strong commitment to curbing current appreciation of yen, investors "won't stop buying" JPY, he says. DPJ presidential race result likely around 0630-0700 GMT. Citibank Japan chief strategy Osamu Takashima also eyeing 83.00 for USD/JPY if Kan wins; adds, halt in recent gains in Europe, U.S. bond yields making USD, EUR less attractive; says Japan exporters also behind USD sales. Meanwhile, EUR/JPY falling as investors take profits after recent surge caused by upward revision of euro-zone GDP growth forecast yesterday, strong China economic data at weekend, says Naoto Taikoji, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . EUR/USD last 107.22. USD/JPY weaker at 83.36, though off 15-year low of 83.25 marked earlier in day. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD rangebound as European bank and U.S. bank selling USD, though major U.K. bank also buying, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in tight 7.7640-7.7690 range near term. "Still, USD/HKD remains downward biased tracking lower USD/CNY, as the freely convertible Hong Kong dollar is often traded as a proxy for the yuan," says trader. Pair last at 7.7675 vs 7.7672 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks succumb to light profit-taking after yesterday's broad-based gains, which pushed STI to new 2010 high. Benchmark off 0.1% at 3062.67, with support tipped at previous year-to-date high of 3043 set in August. Any rebound may be capped at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "Until we get macro news that is consistent, we believe equities markets will be choppy," says DBS Asia strategist Joanne Goh; but notes unless global economic conditions deteriorate to an outright recession, which will force investment portfolios to reduce allocation of riskier assets, "we do not see much downside from current levels." Genting group remains most active in market, with Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG), off 2.9% at US$495, Genting Singapore (G13.SG) flat at S$1.99 after yesterday's rally to multi-year highs. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude steady in electronic trading. Traders on sidelines, awaiting U.S. economic indicators due later today including retail sales, says Daiichi Shohin analyst Koichi Murakami; adds after data from China showed healthy growth, market now watching how U.S. does. "Though China is important, the U.S. is the main driver because emerging countries cannot grow without exporting to the U.S." October Nymex crude last down 1 cent at $77.18/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.4% at 1462.38, fresh 32-month high; technology stocks among biggest advancers, boosted by gains in their U.S. counterparts overnight; dealers say positive market momentum amid resurgent risk appetite to keep KLCI on ascent near-term, targeting 1465 resistance. Among gainers, hard disk drive maker JCY (5161.KU) +7.3% at M