\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Gain, Led By Financials After New Basel Rules\b0\par\par\uc2 2,889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 00:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's stock market is closed Tuesday for a trading holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10544.13  +81.36  +0.78% \par Nasdaq     2285.71  +43.23  +1.93% \par S&P 500    1121.90  +12.35  +1.11% \par FTSE 100   5565.53  +63.89  +1.16% \par Xetra DAX  6261.68  +46.91  +0.75% \par  \par 2Y Tsy 99 22/32  +2/32 0.531% -4.0BP \par 5Y Tsy 98 25/32 +12/32 1.504% -7.8BP \par 10Y Tsy 99 1/32 +16/32 2.735% -6.0BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.63-68     -0.75% \par Range         84.36-83.51 \par EUR/USD       1.2881-82    +1.23% \par Range         1.2892-1.2706 \par AUD/USD       0.9357-59    +0.58% \par Range         0.9362-0.9290 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.60-11.90% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.45-10.85% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    11.65-11.95% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,247.10   +60 cents \par Comex Gold Range    $1,242.30-$1,251.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $77.19   +$0.74 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Financials led U.S. stocks higher Monday as investors welcomed new global banking requirements that will be ushered in gradually enough to ensure banks won't need to scramble to raise capital.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 81.36, or 0.78%, to 10544.13, its biggest gain since Sept. 3. So far this month, the Dow is up 5.29%, its best September start since 1939.\par \par \uc2 Monday, the measure was led by its financial components. Traders were relieved to see the banks likely won't have to raise capital to meet the new rules hammered out in Basel, Switzerland, over the weekend, as central bankers gave the banks nearly a decade to comply with them. \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  rose 1.36, or 3.4%, to 41.12, while \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  gained 40 cents, or 3%, to 13.95 and \uc2 American Express\uc2  added 1.03, or 2.6%, to 41.22. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , not a Dow component, gained 75 cents, or 2.9%, to 26.50\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index rose 12.35, or 1.11%, to 1121.90, its highest close since Aug. 9. Regional banks lifted the S&P 500's financial sector, boosted by Bernstein Research's stock-investment rating on several banks whose under-performance since last April has created a "compelling investment opportunity," according to the firm. \uc2 Zions Bancorp\uc2  climbed 1.47, or 7.4%, to 21.31, while \uc2 Marshall & Ilsley\uc2  gained 31 cents, or 4.5%, to 7.27 and \uc2 Huntington Bancshares\uc2  added 23 cents, or 3.9%, to 6.08.\par \par \uc2 "For banks, the good news is short-term rates are likely to stay low for a while," said John Chisholm, chief investment officer at Acadian Asset Management. "The rates they can charge for loans are still pretty good, and if there's even at the margins just a little bit more demand, that's a good thing for bank earnings," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite gained 43.23, or 1.93%, to 2285.71. The measure's four-day up streak is its longest winning stretch since the seven-day period that ended July 14. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  led a charge among technology stocks. The software giant jumped 1.26, or 5.3%, to 25.11, following a report that the company is planning to sell some of its debt in order to pay dividends to shareholders and finance a buyback of its own stock, according to Bloomberg Television.\par \par \uc2 Apple rose 3.63, or 1.4%, to 267.04 after announcing plans to sell a Wi-Fi-capable version of its popular iPad tablet computer in China beginning Friday.\par \par \uc2 Only the consumer staples sector closed in the red Monday. Food giant \uc2 Sara Lee\uc2  lost 24 cents, or 1.7%, to 14.10, while consumer foods company \uc2 General Mills\uc2  dropped 55 cents, or 1.5%, to 36.64.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Monday after data issued over the weekend showed industrial output growth quickened in August, renewing hopes about the global economic recovery, while inflation was within expectations, which eased concerns about possible new monetary or property tightening measures.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely test the psychological 2750 level this week as a result of the August data.\par \par \uc2 "The data was overall positive for the market, indicating that China's economy is still growing at a healthy pace. The Shanghai index should see a moderate uptrend this week as a result," said Chen Jinren, an analyst at Huatai Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Monday, led by oil shares on rising crude prices, while bank shares also gained on relief that new capital standards have ended up less stringent than previously expected.\par \par \uc2 Market analysts say investors are now focused on Tuesday's presidential race for the ruling Democratic Party of Japan. Powerful fiscal spending advocate Ichiro Ozawa is challenging Prime Minister Naoto Kan for the party leadership in what is seen as a close race.\par \par \uc2 "If Ozawa wins, investors may chase the market higher," said Shinichiro Matsushita, market analyst at Daiwa Securities. "If that happens, it would be one of the few occasions when Japan stocks actually move on politics."\par \par \uc2 The need to help the economy has become a focal point of the election. Analysts say the stock market prefers Ozawa, who is in favor of increasing jobs through public works projects and has called for aggressive spending.\par \par \uc2 An Ozawa win could push the Nikkei to test 9500 Tuesday, market watchers said.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures ended at a one-month high in U.S. trading Monday as the latest evidence of strong economic growth in China boosted the outlook for future oil demand.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese data and a new global banking agreement dulled investors' appetite for safe-haven gold Monday, but the metal still managed to eke out a small gain as not all are convinced that everything is going all right with the world's economy.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed higher Monday as the euro rose against the U.S. dollar and global stock markets gained amid investor demand for riskier assets on the back of the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision's announcement on banking requirements and the Chinese economic data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro Monday notched its biggest one-day gain against the dollar since mid-July as investors bought higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the back of encouraging economic news over the weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Better-than-expected Chinese data; an upward revision to euro-zone growth forecasts; and an agreement on global banking regulations stoked positive investor sentiment. The upbeat sentiment translated into gains in global equities, which also supported the euro and other higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People were pretty well short the euro last week and I think that was the right way to be, but that bet was stopped in its tracks over the weekend," said Jeff Sakamoto, interbank trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Union Bank of California\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Los Angeles. "People were caught wrong-footed--on Sunday, it was a reversal of fortune.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors took the positive weekend news and bid riskier assets sharply higher, with the euro notching its biggest single-session percentage gain against the dollar since July 15, rising about 1.5% on the day, said Brian Dolan, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Particularly well-received by investors was the Basel III announcement with ample time announced for banks not already in compliance with new capital requirements. That means the drumbeat of negativity against the European banking sector has temporarily subsided.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.2873, from $1.2712 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.65 from Y84.21, while the euro was at Y107.66 from Y107.03. The dollar weakened to CHF1.0072 from CHF1.0197. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 81.870 from 82.723.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also sold the dollar for the Swiss franc after Swiss inflation came in slightly higher than expected, increasing chances--in some investors' views--that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise key interest rates to strangle possible inflation at its meeting Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also receded against the yen, coming close to a fresh 15-year low, after cautious investors abandoned the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ahead of Tuesday's election of the head of Japan's ruling party. Some investors speculate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government could be more inclined to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem recent yen strength after the party vote.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Better-than-expected Chinese industrial production figures gave a green light to bid higher the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars, commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that are considered tied to positive global economic activity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With risk returning, speculators are clearly moving back into the commodity-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Matthew Strauss, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian dollar was trading around $0.9357 Monday afternoon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid the positive Chinese economic news, the yuan hit a modern-era high against the U.S. dollar Monday after China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the yuan's reference rate for daily trading at its strongest level against the dollar since it began publishing the daily fixing in 1994.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan's rise Monday, following sharp gains Friday, comes after U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said in an interview Friday that China hasn't done enough to allow the yuan to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys prices rose Monday, reversing losses posted late last week, as market participants remained on edge about the economic recovery and again sought out low-risk U.S. government bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Buyers came back in following three consecutive days of lower prices last week posted after some less-dire bits of U.S. economic data. Overall, though, the economic outlook remains very uncertain, leaving Treasurys in favor. Market participants Monday were setting up for what some believe could be a weaker report on U.S. retail sales for August. The data are scheduled to be released early Tuesday morning. [ 13-09-10 2201GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains in the U.S. government bond market Monday came despite better economic reports from overseas. China, the world's second-largest economy after the U.S., over the weekend reported a stronger-than-expected increase in August industrial production. Retail sales there also rose sharply. The numbers signal that China's economy is growing at a robust pace, calming fears of a sharp downturn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The global economy might be looking okay, but that doesn't necessarily translate into a stronger U.S. economy," said Ira Jersey, director in the U.S. interest-rate strategy team at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In the Treasurys market, "we're seeing a little bit of an unwind from the pessimism that we saw last week," Jersey said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Monday afternoon trade in New York, the 10-year note's price was up 16/32 to 2.735% and the two-year note was up 2/32 to 0.531%. The 30-year bond was up 18/32 to 3.837%. Gains came despite higher stock prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jersey said he believes Treasurys will bounce around a range for a while, with the 10-year yield moving between 2.65% to 2.85%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's year-end projection is for a 10-year yield of 2.65%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys were also up Monday after another Treasury purchase by the Federal Reserve. The Fed bought $3.4 billion in Treasurys maturing August 2016 to May 2020 out of $17.202 billion bids submitted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed Monday also announced its latest Treasury buying schedule, which suggests an average \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  size of about $3 billion, up from the $2 billion average for the previous nine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The first of the new set of purchases will be Wednesday, in the four- to six-year sector. There will be one outright purchase of Treasury Inflation Protected Securities at the end of September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds end higher Monday, reversing an earlier decline after the PBOC set the USD/CNY fixing at a modern-era low, easing concerns over an imminent interest rate hike.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Theoretically speaking, both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation and interest rate increases are measures that can curb inflationary pressure. As Beijing has acted to allow the yuan to pick up its rising pace, the possibility of an interest rate hike is quite low," a Beijing-based analyst at a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index ends is likely to consolidate in a 126.6-126.9 band in the near term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds fell Monday due to speculation that fiscal expansionist Ichiro Ozawa may beat Prime Minister Naoto Kan in the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's leadership race Tuesday, because such a result could lead to further deterioration in the country's woeful fiscal health.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say an Ozawa win would be bad news for JGB investors because the DPJ's Lower House of Parliament majority means the party leadership winner will almost certainly become Japan's next prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ozawa has been making repeated promises that, as prime minister, he would fulfill costly pledges from last year's general election--pledges from which Kan had stepped back, and which would almost certainly entail more government borrowing, saturating the JGB market. Ozawa has also said he would not hesitate to increase JGB issuance if necessary to compile economic stimulus packages.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such prospects prompted some investors Monday to sell long-term bonds while most market participants decided to wait for the election result before making any moves, said RuiXue Xu, rates strategist at RBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also important for the JGB market Tuesday is a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  auction of Y1.1 trillion of 20-year bonds with an expected coupon of 1.9%--0.3 percentage point higher than the previous 20-year offering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the higher coupon, analysts forecast a poor showing. One reason, said Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome, is that it is hard to bid aggressively ahead of the DPJ leadership race.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The auction result will be released at 0345 GMT, while the DPJ election result is expected to become available after 0600 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par H-P To Buy Arcsight For $1.5B \par Geithner Urges Passage Of Small Business Bill \par Banks Get New Restraints \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Microsoft\f28 \uc2 \f2  Said To Plan Debt Sale \par Us Posts $90.53B Budget Deficit In August \par Harrisburg Pa., Sued Over Missed Payments \par Analyst Mayo Suggests Citi Should Be Broken Up \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 GE Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  Launches $4B Bond Offering \par Bank Of America-Merrill Sees Euro Trading Higher \par China Premier: Economic Stimulus Program Timely, Necessary; Risks Remain \par =Apple Unveils Plans To Sell Wi-Fi Version Of IPad In China \par =WSJ:Toyota Plans 6 New Hybrids By 2012,Fuel Cell Model In 2015 \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  Jul Retail Trade Survey \par 0100  S Korea      2Q  Non-financial company outstanding debt \par 0130  Australia    Aug NAB Business Survey \par 0300  New Zealand  Aug NZ Government bonds held for non-residents \par 0400  EU           Aug European Monster Employment Index \par 0430  Japan        Jul Revised Industrial Production \par 0430  Japan        Jul Revised Retail Sales \par 0530  France       Aug CPI \par 0600  Germany      Aug WPI \par 0630  India        Aug Monthly WPI (all commodities) \par 0830  Hong Kong    2Q  Gross National Product & External Factor Income \par                    Flows \par 0830  UK           Aug UK monthly inflation figures published \par 0830  UK           Jul DCLG House Price Index \par 0900  Germany      Sep ZEW Indicator of Economic Sentiment \par 0900  EU           Jul Industrial Production \par 0900  EU           2Q  Labour cost index \par 100