\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Posts Biggest 1-Day Gain Vs Dollar In 2 Mos\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 22:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro Monday notched its biggest one-day gain against the dollar since mid-July as investors bought higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of encouraging economic news over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Better-than-expected Chinese data; an upward revision to euro-zone growth forecasts; and an agreement on global banking regulations stoked positive investor sentiment. The upbeat sentiment translated into gains in global equities, which also supported the euro and other higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "People were pretty well short the euro last week and I think that was the right way to be, but that bet was stopped in its tracks over the weekend," said Jeff Sakamoto, interbank trader at \uc2 Union Bank of California\uc2  in Los Angeles. "People were caught wrong-footed--on Sunday, it was a reversal of fortune.\par \par \uc2 Investors took the positive weekend news and bid riskier assets sharply higher, with the euro notching its biggest single-session percentage gain against the dollar since July 15, rising about 1.5% on the day, said Brian Dolan, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\par \par \uc2 To see the euro's performance against the dollar, please see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4591"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4591}}}\par \par \uc2 Particularly well-received by investors was the Basel III announcement with ample time announced for banks not already in compliance with new capital requirements. That means the drumbeat of negativity against the European banking sector has temporarily subsided.\par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon, the euro was at $1.2873, from $1.2712 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.65 from Y84.21, while the euro was at Y107.66 from Y107.03. The dollar weakened to CHF1.0072 from CHF1.0197. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 81.870 from 82.723.\par \par \uc2 Investors also sold the dollar for the Swiss franc after Swiss inflation came in slightly higher than expected, increasing chances--in some investors' views--that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  could raise key interest rates to strangle possible inflation at its meeting Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also receded against the yen, coming close to a fresh 15-year low, after cautious investors abandoned the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of Tuesday's election of the head of Japan's ruling party. Some investors speculate the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government could be more inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem recent yen strength after the party vote.\par \par \uc2 Better-than-expected Chinese industrial production figures gave a green light to bid higher the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars, commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are considered tied to positive global economic activity.\par \par \uc2 Speculators were raising their bets on the Australian dollar even before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached a four-month high against the dollar last week, according to data released late Friday by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission. The New Zealand and Canadian dollars also garnered increased interest from speculative traders in the most recent Commitments of Traders' report, but were outpaced by their Australian counterpart. Positive bets on the Australian dollar increased by about 13,000 contracts, or about 30%, to reach a total of 56,900 in the week ended September 7, according to the data.\par \par \uc2 "With risk returning, speculators are clearly moving back into the commodity-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Matthew Strauss, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was trading around $0.9357 Monday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Amid the positive Chinese economic news, the yuan hit a modern-era high against the U.S. dollar Monday after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan's reference rate for daily trading at its strongest level against the dollar since it began publishing the daily fixing in 1994.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's rise Monday, following sharp gains Friday, comes after U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said in an interview Friday that China hasn't done enough to allow the yuan to rise.\par \par \uc2 With the ICE Dollar Index weakening, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bearish exchange-traded fund was up 1.16% from late Friday, while its PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bullish was down 1.08%. The two exchange-traded funds are based on \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures indexes, whose composition mirrors that of the ICE's Dollar Index.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Bradley Davis in New York, Don Curren in Toronto, and Aaron Back and Liu Li in Beijing contributed to this article.) [ 09-13-10 1609ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \p