\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of America-Merrill Revises Euro Forecast Higher Vs Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 20:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Bank of America-Merrill Lynch on Monday revised higher its forecast for the euro against the dollar, calling for the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drop, but to drop less than originally predicted.\par \par \uc2 The euro will trade at $1.20 by the end of 2010, the bank said, revised upward from its earlier forecast of $1.15.\par \par \uc2 "The downward revision to the U.S. growth outlook reinforces our bullish [dollar] view for the remainder of the year," BofA \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  strategists said. Investors often turn to the safe-harbor dollar when they worry about global growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon, the euro traded at $1.2873 on the heels of positive Chinese economic data and an upward revision to euro-zone growth forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rebounded strongly from its four-year low of $1.1876 hit in early June, when investors worried the region's sovereign-debt crisis would infect its financial sector. Since then, bank stress tests and better-than-expected euro-zone data have allayed some investor fears.\par \par \uc2 But "continued hangover from the sovereign-debt crisis," as issuance of government debt picks up steam in the fourth quarter of 2010, along with the need for increased competitiveness in peripheral countries, such as Greece, whose growth is likely to slow under the weight of belt-tightening budget programs, means the euro is likely to trade lower against the dollar, the analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Still, the euro will not fall below its June low as the situations facing the monetary union do "not require another round of crisis-like sentiment."\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also trade at $1.20 by the end of 2011, according to the latest BofA Merrill forecast, based "on a pickup in global growth and prospects of tighter monetary policy in Europe," the analysts said. Its previous forecast called for the euro to trade at $1.10 by the end of 2011.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar will drop against the yen, trading at Y79 by the end of 2010, the analysts said, revising their forecast from an original year-end target of Y81.\par \par \uc2 "Low U.S. interest rates, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand and domestic exporter interest should put downward pressure" on the pair, the BofA Merrill analysts said. "In addition, we expect politics to have an impact on the yen." The election for the leader of Japan's ruling party--considered a key gauge as to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets--is Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon, the dollar traded at Y83.62, down from Y84.21 late Friday, within striking distance of its 15-year low of Y83.34 hit in early September.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com [ 09-13-10 1408ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100913e69d000bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
