 Thursday's close. Markets were shut Friday for religious holiday. "The strong data has raised some degree of expectations of a rate hike at the policy review on September 16," says dealer. Tips, 10-year paper yields in 7.93%-7.97% in session. India's July industrial production expanded by 13.8% on-year vs 7.2% year ago and sharply above 7.8% median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 19 economists. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0235 GMT misstated the extent of the market high. The correct version is as follows:) S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 3-month high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan stocks up sharply across board; Taiex last +1.2% at 7986.51, but meets strong resistance at 8000 after trading in 7946.98-7995.35 range in morning. Capital Securities' analyst Diana Wu says Taiex already broke immediate resistance at 7950, as investor sentiment buoyed by ECFA (trade pact with China) which took effect Sunday; tips China plays, textile, financial stocks to outperform broader market. China Airlines (2610.TW) +4.4% at NT$20.10 ahead of officially joining \uc2 SkyTeam\uc2  Alliance Tuesday, smaller rival \uc2 EVA Airways\uc2  (2618.TW) also +5.2% at NT$24.10. Hon Hai (2317.TW) +3.24% after posting August revenue doubled from year earlier, TSMC (2330.TW) also +1.4% at NT$60.10. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 4-week high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Geithner's remark made in WSJ interview Friday that China has not done enough in terms of CNY appreciation presents another hurdle to Japan stepping in to slow rising JPY says Hideki Amikura, senior dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking. "Even for a solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you need at least an O.K. sign from the country of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  you plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in, in this case the U.S." But getting such go-ahead from U.S. almost impossible, he says, given slowing U.S. economic growth and U.S. likely to want its exports to remain competitive. Adds, "Geithner's remarks are our fresh reasoning why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Geithner, in WSJ interview, says China has not done enough to allow CNY to rise, after Beijing signaling in June that they are going to let the exchange rate start to reflect market forces. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.0% at 9331.22 midday, as big banks lead gainers after Basel Committee's new capital adequacy ratio rules were less stringent than expected, traders say; 9400 index resistance level may remain for rest of day. "Some investors are staying on the sidelines before the Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election tomorrow (TSE 1st section volume just 700 million shares), but overall sentiment is good today," says Hikaru Sato, senior technical analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. MUFG (8306.TO) up 2.2% at Y412, \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 +1.5% at Y132. Shippers also higher, with \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) up 2.7% to Y346 on Nikkei report that maritime shipping rates for iron ore hitting 3-month high. Slightly weaker yen benefiting exporters generally; \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 2.9% at Y4,270, Honda (7267.TO) up 1.9% at Y2,838. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +1.2% at 21,509.02 tracking Wall Street cues, but further upside eyed capped at 22,000 near term. Taifook notes strong China August CPI data released at weekend well-anticipated, not having impact on local stocks; says "the broader market's key features would continue to be rotational buying and range trading in the near term." Among leading gainers, HKEx (0388.HK) +1.6% at HK$136.70 as local bourse extends recent strength, while Cnooc (0883.HK) +2.0% at HK$14.42 on higher oil prices. Volume brisk at HK$9.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower after PBOC adviser Li Daokui says on Sunday that China ought to raise deposit interest rates slightly, following strong August CPI data. "Since last week, there has been speculation among investors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might hike interest rates imminently to curb inflation. Such concerns have hurt the overall sentiment in the local bond market," says Shanghai-based trader with local brokerage. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.64 vs 126.67 last close, tipped to test short-term support of 126.5 in coming sessions. (ming.wang@dowjones.com) [ 09-12-10 2310ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100913e69d00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
