\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Up 1.2%; Extends Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 3,188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 04:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 3-month high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Corrected September 13, 2010 23:02 ET (03:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Geithner's remark made in WSJ interview Friday that China has not done enough in terms of CNY appreciation presents another hurdle to Japan stepping in to slow rising JPY says Hideki Amikura, senior dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking. "Even for a solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you need at least an O.K. sign from the country of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  you plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in, in this case the U.S." But getting such go-ahead from U.S. almost impossible, he says, given slowing U.S. economic growth and U.S. likely to want its exports to remain competitive. Adds, "Geithner's remarks are our fresh reasoning why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Geithner, in WSJ interview, says China has not done enough to allow CNY to rise, after Beijing signaling in June that they are going to let the exchange rate start to reflect market forces. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.0% at 9331.22 midday, as big banks lead gainers after Basel Committee's new capital adequacy ratio rules were less stringent than expected, traders say; 9400 index resistance level may remain for rest of day. "Some investors are staying on the sidelines before the Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election tomorrow (TSE 1st section volume just 700 million shares), but overall sentiment is good today," says Hikaru Sato, senior technical analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. MUFG (8306.TO) up 2.2% at Y412, \uc2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \uc2 +1.5% at Y132. Shippers also higher, with \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) up 2.7% to Y346 on Nikkei report that maritime shipping rates for iron ore hitting 3-month high. Slightly weaker yen benefiting exporters generally; \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 2.9% at Y4,270, Honda (7267.TO) up 1.9% at Y2,838. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +1.2% at 21,509.02 tracking Wall Street cues, but further upside eyed capped at 22,000 near term. Taifook notes strong China August CPI data released at weekend well-anticipated, not having impact on local stocks; says "the broader market's key features would continue to be rotational buying and range trading in the near term." Among leading gainers, HKEx (0388.HK) +1.6% at HK$136.70 as local bourse extends recent strength, while Cnooc (0883.HK) +2.0% at HK$14.42 on higher oil prices. Volume brisk at HK$9.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower after PBOC adviser Li Daokui says on Sunday that China ought to raise deposit interest rates slightly, following strong August CPI data. "Since last week, there has been speculation among investors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might hike interest rates imminently to curb inflation. Such concerns have hurt the overall sentiment in the local bond market," says Shanghai-based trader with local brokerage. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.64 vs 126.67 last close, tipped to test short-term support of 126.5 in coming sessions. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds lower, weighed by weaker U.S. Treasurys Friday, Taiex's 1.2% gain, says local brokerage trader. Still, notes volume thin as traders stay on sidelines ahead of new 10-year bond auction Wednesday. Expects weak demand at 10-year bond auction as "Taiwan is likely to hike key policy rates again this month, and also on recent strong stock market." Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet September 30. 10-year yield last at 1.2415% in when-issued trade vs 1.2253% yesterday, eyed in 1.2350%-1.2550% band for day. 5-year yield last at 0.8950% vs 0.8850% yesterday, eyed in 0.8850%-0.9050% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian banks should be able to comfortably meet common equity tier 1 and total tier 1 requirements coming from Basel committee, though questions remain, says Macquarie research. Adds Australian banks tier 1 ratios will likely be at least 10% under new guidelines, with banks able to support lower levels of credit growth as a result. But notes "uncertainty remains as to whether or not banks will modify payouts to avoid a situation where they could eat into the capital conservation buffer." Next up locally, APRA will take next year to work through proposals and adapt for Australia. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares set new record high in early trade, playing catch up from Friday's gains on Wall Street (local markets closed Friday for holiday); benchmark PSEi +1.1% at 3942.07 in moderate volume; immediate resistance 3950, then 4000, support at 3900. Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities, says foreign fund inflows due to strong corporate, economic outlook continue to drive market to uncharted territory. "The peso's appreciation is also helping sentiment," he adds. Advancers led by property, utility stocks such as PLDT (TEL.PH) +0.6% at PHP2,420, Megaworld (MEG.PH) +4.7% at PHP2.23, SM Investments (SM.PH) +1.2% at PHP516, Filinvest Land (FLI.PH) +2.9% at PHP1.40 and \uc2 DMCI Holdings\uc2  (DMC.PH) +2.7% at PHP26.30. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares choppy, last +0.1% at 451.93, off earlier low at 448.97. Despite bouncing off lows, many players remain "hesitant to enter the market to buy now because of a lack of fresh news on how the government will act to support the stock market," says trader. But traders predict key index to end in positive teritorry today. Tip index to move in 445-455 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW trims losses on bargain hunting, importer buying after slipping to 5-week low of 1,158.0 earlier, while players remain wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, traders say. Pair now back above 1,160 mark at 1,163.4, though still tad down vs 1,165.7 late Friday in Seoul amid Kospi's 0.5% gain. Local bank trader puts cap at 1,165. Says, chances high pair could head back down in afternoon trade on exporter USD selling, anticipated fund-inflows related to foreigners' active buying in local stocks. Traders put initial support at 1,160, then 1,155. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY long non-commercial positions vs USD rose to 52,183 contracts in week to September 7 vs 49,904 contracts week earlier, showing trend-followers may begin reducing JPY long positions in near future as investors are known to be unwilling to pile up JPY positions too far from 50,000. Daisuke Karakama, senior economist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  says as U.S.-Japan bond yields gap widening, U.S. economic indicators such as retail sales data due tomorrow may give market cue to square their positions if those indicators turn out better than expected. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower, keeping in step with weaker USD vs EUR, other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising on risk appetite after China industrial data; pair last at 43.94 -- first time below 44 since Aug. 7, 2008 -- after settling at 44.12 late Thursday; support at 43.75, resistance 44.30; "The sentiment is really bullish for the peso and we're getting help from strong Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 09-12-10 2235ET ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0235 GMT misstated the extent of the market high. The correct version is as follows:) S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 3-month high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan stocks up sharply across board; Taiex last +1.2% at 7986.51, but meets strong resistance at 8000 after trading in 7946.98-7995.35 range in morning. Capital Securities' analyst Diana Wu says Taiex already broke immediate resistance at 7950, as investor sentiment buoyed by ECFA (trade pact with China) which took effect Sunday; tips China plays, textile, financial stocks to outperform broader market. China Airlines (2610.TW) +4.4% at NT$20.10 ahead of officially joining \f28 \uc2 \f2 SkyTeam\f28 \uc2 \f2  Alliance Tuesday, smaller rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 EVA Airways\f28 \uc2 \f2  (2618.TW) also +5.2% at NT$24.10. Hon Hai (2317.TW) +3.24% after posting August revenue doubled from year earlier, TSMC (2330.TW) also +1.4% at NT$60.10. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.2% at 4616.2, extends gains to 4-week high of 4619.2 after finding support on dips to 4600.0 as strong China economic data added to bullish leads from Wall Street. "Equities are obviously taking comfort from Chinese data over the weekend and optimism is creeping into the market," says RBS Head of Sydney Sales Justin Gallagher. "Basel III (capital adequacy rules) are a long way off and are not going to cause any problems for Australian banks because they are well capitalized." Expects U.S. economic data, including U.S. retail sales and industrial production, to be key this week. "If we continue to see supportive macroeconomic data, I think September could well be a very good month for equity markets globally. But if we trip over a few of these numbers, we will be back to where we were a few weeks ago." Adds that S&P/ASX 200 would need to close near 4650 to convince the market that major technical resistance around 4600 has broken in sustained fashion. Expects a quick 200 point gain if that occurs. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Geithner's remark made in WSJ interview Friday that China has not done enough in terms of CNY appreciation presents another hurdle to Japan stepping in to slow rising JPY says Hideki Amikura, senior dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking. "Even for a solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , you need at least an O.K. sign from the country of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  you plan to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in, in this case the U.S." But getting such go-ahead from U.S. almost impossible, he says, given slowing U.S. economic growth and U.S. likely to want its exports to remain competitive. Adds, "Geithner's remarks are our fresh reasoning why \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." Geithner, in WSJ interview, says China has not done enough to allow CNY to rise, after Beijing signaling in June that they are going to let the exchange rate start to reflect market forces. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.0% at 9331.22 midday, as big banks lead gainers after Basel Committee's new capital adequacy ratio rules were less stringent than expected, traders say; 9400 index resistance level may remain for rest of day. "Some investors are staying on the sidelines before the Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) presidential election tomorrow (TSE 1st section volume just 700 million shares), but overall sentiment is good today," says Hikaru Sato, senior technical analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. MUFG (8306.TO) up 2.2% at Y412, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho FG (8411.TO) \f28 \uc2 \f2 +1.5% at Y132. Shippers also higher, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Yusen\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9101.TO) up 2.7% to Y346 on Nikkei report that maritime shipping rates for iron ore hitting 3-month high. Slightly weaker yen benefiting exporters generally; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electron\f28 \uc2 \f2  (8035.TO) up 2.9% at Y4,270, Honda (7267.TO) up 1.9% at Y2,838. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +1.2% at 21,509.02 tracking Wall Street cues, but further upside eyed capped at 22,000 near term. Taifook notes strong China August CPI data released at weekend well-anticipated, not having impact on local stocks; says "the broader market's key features would continue to be rotational buying and range trading in the near term." Among leading gainers, HKEx (0388.HK) +1.6% at HK$136.70 as local bourse extends recent strength, while Cnooc (0883.HK) +2.0% at HK$14.42 on higher oil prices. Volume brisk at HK$9.56 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower after PBOC adviser Li Daokui says on Sunday that China ought to raise deposit interest rates slightly, following strong August CPI data. "Since last week, there has been speculation among investors that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might hike interest rates imminently to curb inflation. Such concerns have hurt the overall sentiment in the local bond market," says Shanghai-based trader with local brokerage. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.64 vs 126.67 last close, tipped to test short-term support of 126.5 in coming sessions. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds lower, weighed by weaker U.S. Treasurys Friday, Taiex's 1.2% gain, says local brokerage trader. Still, notes volume thin as traders stay on sidelines ahead of new 10-year bond auction Wednesday. Expects weak demand at 10-year bond auction as "Taiwan is likely to hike key policy rates again this month, and also on recent strong stock market." Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to meet September 30. 10-year yield last at 1.2415% in when-issued trade vs 1.2253% yesterday, eyed in 1.2350%-1.2550% band for day. 5-year yield last at 0.8950% vs 0.8850% yesterday, eyed in 0.8850%-0.9050% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian banks should be able to comfortably meet common equity tier 1 and total tier 1 requirements coming from Basel committee, though questions remain, says Macquarie research. Adds Australian banks tier 1 ratios will likely be at least 10% under new guidelines, with banks able to support lower levels of credit growth as a result. But notes "uncertainty remains as to whether or not banks will modify payouts to avoid a situation where they could eat into the capital conservation buffer." Next up locally, APRA will take next year to work through proposals and adapt for Australia. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares set new record high in early trade, playing catch up from Friday's gains on Wall Street (local markets closed Friday for holiday); benchmark PSEi +1.1% at 3942.07 in moderate volume; immediate resistance 3950, then 4000, support at 3900. Erwin Balita, research head at SB Equities, says foreign fund inflows due to strong corporate, economic outlook continue to drive market to uncharted territory. "The peso's appreciation is also helping sentiment," he adds. Advancers led b